now I get it all.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:46.150
Anyway.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.640
So that's just the spirit.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.310
Let's see, any questions
about last time?

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.500
Otherwise, we'll continue on--

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.760
may I remind you this week
is continuing our slog

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.810
through the math.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.970
It will finish pretty
much on Friday.

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.180
So already next week, the
sun is going to come out.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.650
And if you've survived,
it'll start getting actually

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.680
very interesting.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.340
So, OK, any-- no questions?

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.220
On that case, what
I'll do is let me just

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.630
remind you where we were.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.150
Last time we talked
about the idea

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:25.560
of defining an inequality in
terms of a cone, a proper cone.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.530
And so that's a
general construction.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.900
The truth is we really
mostly do this only

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.400
with two specific cones.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.500
One is a non-negative
orthant, and that allows

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.450
us to compare two vectors.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.460
And basically, one vector
is less than another

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.970
when the corresponding entries
are less than the other.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.240
And the other one is the
positive semidefinite cone,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:50.730
and that allows us to compare
two symmetric matrices.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.800
And you say that one
is bigger than another.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.960
If the difference is
positive, semidefinite.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.540
And I'm presuming you've
seen this somewhere.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.850
So, I don't think
these two can--

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.490
they shouldn't be
completely brand new.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.900
So, what I mentioned
at the end of last time

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.020
is something quite interesting,
that the idea of when you

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.240
generalize an inequality, the--

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.370
I mean, just the fact that
the symbol we're using

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.890
and the name we're using,
which is inequality,

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.100
like all powerful notation,
it should remind you

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.580
of inequalities
among real numbers.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.455
That's sort of the idea, right?

00:03:26.455 --> 00:03:28.330
I mean, it's the same
way when you write down

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.490
ax equals b, where
that's a matrix equation.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.220
And somebody looks
at it and said, wait,

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.860
I solved problems like
that in third grade.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.650
The response is, yeah,
but that's a 2,000

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.320
by 1,000 matrix there
or something like that.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.030
So it's the same idea.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.275
And the idea of this
generalization is that many

00:03:47.275 --> 00:03:49.540
things you know are true,
many things-- sorry,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.965
many things in the old context
remain true and some don't.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.340
If everything
remained true, then

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.590
it's really not even
a generalization,

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.170
you're just saying it now
applies to more things.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:02.560
And so the main
thing that you don't

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.800
have when you are looking
at vector inequalities

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.140
is it's not a linear
or total ordering.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.590
And what that means is
you can have two things,

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.650
and it is false that this
one is less than that

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.700
and the other way around.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.970
Now, for real numbers,
that's impossible.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.649
Because either one
real number is either

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:21.910
less than or equal to the
other or the other way

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:22.990
around, period.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.810
And so that's called a
linear or total ordering.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.880
And you don't have
that with vectors.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.092
And this is obvious.

00:04:29.092 --> 00:04:31.300
Actually, we're going to
see later that this actually

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.880
has a lot of interesting
practical implications

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.410
that we will get to.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.900
So one thing that
happens then is

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:42.410
everyone knows what the minimum
is of a bunch of numbers.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:46.030
And by the way, I might
as well mention it now,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.090
when you have an
infinite set of numbers,

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.640
the min is replaced with
something called the infimum.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.380
If you know about
this, that's fine.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:55.660
If you don't, think
of it as the min.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.870
It is just the min, but it
applies to infinite sets, OK?

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.710
So that's really pretty
much all you need to know.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:03.280
OK.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:11.640
So, what happens with a vector
here, when vector inequalities,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.710
is that the idea of min
or infimum or minimum,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.870
or maximum for that
matter, actually

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.930
splits into two concepts
which are very different.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:28.380
One is still called the
minimum, and the other one

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.220
is called actually minimal.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.450
And so these are just
two different ideas.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.210
And we'll see actually
later that this

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.940
is going to come up in
various applications

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.440
and be interesting.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.010
So, minimum means
that it's a point

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.400
in the set with the
following property,

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.260
that everything
else in that set is

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.890
comparable and larger than
or equal to you with respect

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:54.120
to that inequality.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.670
That's what minimum is, right?

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.615
It's not hard to show that
there's a unique minimum.

00:06:00.615 --> 00:06:02.740
In other words, you can't
have two different points

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:03.630
which are both--

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.570
well, before you show it,
you'd have to say, "A minimum."

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.160
And then after you
show it, you're

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.470
allowed to say "The minimum."

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.570
Then there's the idea
of a minimal element.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.990
And a minimal element is
one, like here's an example.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:22.210
X2 is minimal for this set S2,
and you'd say, with respect

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.820
to the non-negative orthant.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:28.030
And what that means is, I
mean, very roughly speaking,

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.120
it means that there's
no point in this

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:37.930
set here, which is less than
or equal to x2, except itself.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.080
And so that's shown
visually here.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.650
You can also see that all
of these points are minimal.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.980
Everything on this
line is minimal.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.190
How about a point there?

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.650
It's not minimal
because there would

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.505
be points, which are
comparable, that are in the set,

00:06:55.505 --> 00:06:57.630
comparable and less than
or equal to, and not equal

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:58.980
to that point.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.000
So that's one thing.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.905
And this, you'll just
have go to a quiet place

00:07:02.905 --> 00:07:03.780
and think about this.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:06.150
And you'll get lots of
chances throughout the course

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.030
to get this straight.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.275
There's a question.

00:07:08.275 --> 00:07:10.800
[INAUDIBLE] with a minimal
would be with respect

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.120
to a different cone?

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.050
Yes.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.290
So, when we talk about
minimum and minimal,

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.050
it is always with respect
to a specific cone.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.630
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.130
Yeah.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:23.870
No, then it would
not be this, right?

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:25.310
So if I had a different cone--

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:28.580
like, let me just draw down
a different cone in R2.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:29.780
This is an R2.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:31.970
So here's a different cone.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.760
It's this little thing here.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.250
That's a cone, right, in R2.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.538
And then we could ask, is that
point minimal with respect

00:07:41.538 --> 00:07:42.080
to that cone?

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:45.890
Do you want to make a guess?

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:50.890
No.

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:51.460
Oh, it is.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:51.970
It is?

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:52.540
Yeah.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.510
Because I'll just reproduce
that set a bit here.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.470
It looks like this.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.970
The set looks kind of like that.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:07:59.080
Here's the point.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.910
And what you want
to do is you want

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.580
to look at all the
points that are less than

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:07.120
or equal to this point
with respect to this cone.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.805
And that basically
looks like this.

00:08:08.805 --> 00:08:10.180
Let's see if I
can do this right.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.510
It's going to be all
these points here.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:17.110
And then we want to intersect
that set with the set

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:22.880
S, which is here, and find
out if the only point is

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:24.080
this point.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.400
And it is.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.240
So it is-- so with
respect to that cone,

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.600
that point x2 is still minimal.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.880
OK.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.693
Now we'll get to
something called

00:08:38.693 --> 00:08:40.110
the separating
hyperplane theorem.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.780
It's actually super famous.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.022
And I mean, it looks
kind of innocent.

00:08:45.022 --> 00:08:46.980
And you can actually make
a reasonable argument

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.650
that everything we're going
to do is based only on this.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:53.670
So, it's not bad to have a
single visual picture that

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.950
tells you everything
you need to know.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.930
And what it says is this.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:04.140
It says that if I have
two convex sets right

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.220
which do not intersect-- that's
what it means to be disjoint,

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.070
so I have disjoint convex
sets-- then it says,

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.770
very roughly speaking, I can
stick a hyperplane between.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.810
I can separate
them by hyperplane.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.200
And you may have seen this in
some machine learning class,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.050
where you were talking about
two different sets of points

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:23.628
being linearly separable.

00:09:23.628 --> 00:09:25.170
That's how people
would say it there.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.360
I mean, if you haven't,
that's fine, too.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.040
So this is the picture.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:35.880
If there's a set C and a set
D, then there is some non-zero

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:41.670
vector a and a number b so
that if a is pointing this way,

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.641
it says that--

00:09:43.641 --> 00:09:45.630
this half space
here is everywhere

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:47.750
where a transpose
x is less than b,

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.980
and C is entirely in that set.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.770
This half space
here is everywhere

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.350
where a transpose x is bigger
than or equal to b over here,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.900
and D lies entirely in that set.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.490
So that's the idea.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.790
Right now this has
no, I mean, this

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:06.877
doesn't have any context
or anything like that.

00:10:06.877 --> 00:10:09.210
But we'll see later it's
related to a lot of stuff we're

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.550
going to see by about week 5.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:16.020
I mean, it is the basis, for
example, of a lot of economics,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:16.620
period.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.698
Because basically
you'll see later,

00:10:19.698 --> 00:10:21.990
this will correspond-- you'll
say something like, well,

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.490
there's no arbitrage.

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.990
And that's going to turn out
to be a picture like this.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:27.240
I mean, not in two dimensions.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:28.840
But nevertheless,
it's going to be.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.678
So we'll see that
stuff like this,

00:10:30.678 --> 00:10:32.220
although right now
it has no context,

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.770
it's going to come up
over and over again.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.510
There's a gazillion
variations on this

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:42.607
like when is this true when
the inequalities are strict?

00:10:42.607 --> 00:10:44.440
And then you say they're
strictly separated.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.930
And it gets complicated
and all that sort of stuff.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:50.200
You would see maybe one or
two variations in the book,

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.790
but you actually don't even--

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.290
I don't think you
need to know them.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:57.010
You just need to have
this picture in your head.

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:00.700
Now, showing this is
actually not too hard.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:01.810
We're not going to do it.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.610
I mean, it's sort
of like an exercise,

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.067
and we'll see things more
sophisticated anyway.

00:11:06.067 --> 00:11:07.900
So that's the separating
hyperplane theorem.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:08.860
Yes?

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.720
What does singleton mean?

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:11.530
Oh, a singleton.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:12.030
Yeah.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.560
Oh, by the way,
thank you for asking.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.950
Please ask about anything
that's not clear.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.200
I try.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.750
But that is high
BBC mathematics.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.260
It means a set with
exactly one thing in it.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:27.400
So that's a singleton.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.320
And that's completely standard.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:31.150
So, I mean, maybe not
in different fields,

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.505
but in high BBC math.

00:11:33.505 --> 00:11:35.380
That's why everyone
should speak a little bit

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:36.730
of fancy high BBC math.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.320
You should understand
a little bit,

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:41.770
you should recognize it
and actually speak some.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:43.603
I mean, if your
accent-free, that's awesome.

00:11:43.603 --> 00:11:45.895
Then you can hang out with
mathematicians or something.

00:11:45.895 --> 00:11:47.750
But I mean, if
that's a good thing.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:49.190
I don't know.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:49.870
Anyway.

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:52.210
OK.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.405
And there's variations on it.

00:11:54.405 --> 00:11:55.780
And this is an
important concept.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.670
We'll get to this later as well.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.097
There's the supporting
hyperplane theorem.

00:12:01.097 --> 00:12:03.430
So first, I have to say what
a supporting hyperplane is.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:05.222
So a supporting hyperplane
is I have a set,

00:12:05.222 --> 00:12:06.440
it doesn't have to be convex.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:10.780
And I have a point on the
boundary of the set here.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:12.880
And if you don't know
what the boundary is,

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.010
we could just take
that to be a completely

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.530
informal that your
eyeball will tell you

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:18.800
what's on the boundary.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.300
So that's the point
on the boundary.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:24.610
And then a supporting
hyperplane is a hyperplane

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.640
that, very roughly speaking,
is tangent to the set here.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.370
And what it means is it
passes through this point,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:33.370
but everything in this half
space is not in this set.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:36.130
Or another way to
say it is everything

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.890
this hyperplane tells
you that this set is

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:41.680
on in one of the half spaces.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.360
That's what it tells you.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.640
That's the supporting
hyperplane.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:48.640
And what it says is if
you have a convex set,

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:52.220
then there exists a supporting
hyperplane at every point.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:55.970
And that's not too, I mean,
it's kind of intuitive

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.880
that this would be the case.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.950
The picture would be
something like this.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:08.240
Here's a convex set
C. And let's see,

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.960
let's look at some
boundary points.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.890
So if you take a
point there, there

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.720
is indeed a
supporting hyperplane.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.680
And it's basically just--
it's tangent to this set.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:20.570
By the way, what happens
if I go to a corner?

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.230
Suppose there's a corner there.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.510
Let's talk about
supporting hyperplanes.

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:29.310
Are there some?

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.320
Is there more than one?

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:30.820
Yes.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:31.600
Precisely.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:32.350
That's it.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:36.580
So when you have a kink
in a set, there's one.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:41.880
Is there a supporting hyperplane
to this set at this point?

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:42.660
No.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:43.230
No.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:48.270
So, that's what it
means, is that there

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:52.800
is no hyperplane at that point
that supports it at that point

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.410
and has those set
on one side of it.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.150
But by the way, the
converse is also true.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.120
If a set has a
supporting hyperplane

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.460
at every point of its
boundary, then it's convex.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.550
But, again, if you care about
these things, that's fine.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.830
But a lot of it
is just, I think--

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.530
you should think of
this intuitively.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.280
And these things are
just going to come up

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:16.560
over and over in applications,
which will be really cool.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:17.190
OK.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:20.580
Next weird totally
nonmotivated thing

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:23.550
we're going to do this
week is dual cones

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.030
and generalized inequality.

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:28.890
So, the first is--

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:31.200
so, actually, let me
just start, let me

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.870
explain what this is
a generalization of.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:41.100
So you may know in linear
algebra, if that's a subspace,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.730
then you have V perp.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.440
That's how you pronounce this.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:50.830
And that's the set of all
x for which y transpose

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:58.300
x equals 0 for all y
in V. So that's it.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.550
So, that's a useful concept.

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:02.260
It's a set of all
things orthogonal

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:03.940
to everything in your subspace.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.710
So that's what-- and
it kind of means--

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:07.660
it's also called the
orthogonal complement,

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:08.870
is another name for this.

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:11.380
It's got a bunch of names.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.840
And probably, you
may even know how

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.690
to compute a basis
for it if I gave you

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:20.230
a basis for, let's
say, V or something.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:26.300
OK, so this is the same concept,
except now with inequalities.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.440
In fact, in some sense, that's
all we're doing mathematically,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.560
is we're doing linear
algebra with asymmetries.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.360
So we replace equalities
with inequalities.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.550
That's actually all we're doing.

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:40.810
OK, so let's look at what
it means to be a dual cone.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:43.780
All right, sorry that I
switched x and y over there

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:45.730
and here, but that's fine.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:48.280
So let's actually just
draw some pictures,

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.200
because I think
that's really the best

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:54.220
way to understand this.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.720
OK, so let me write
down the definition

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.850
so we have it, which
is K star equals

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:03.640
and, I mean, it's
perfectly OK for me

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:07.840
not to do this to get it right.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:13.060
This is for all x
in K. There we go.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:16.480
So it says you have to make
a non-negative inner product

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.210
with every entry of the cone.

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:19.870
Cool?

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:20.370
OK.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.420
So I'll just do a
couple and then we quit,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:25.690
and I let you go off and do
this yourself, all right?

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:29.740
So let's write down some
cones Oh, look, here's a cone.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:31.900
Ready?

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:33.130
Hey, it's a singleton.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:33.830
There we go.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:34.840
So that's a cone.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:36.820
It's a stupid cone,
but it's a cone.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:41.950
And you tell me, what is
the dual cone of this?

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:42.990
R2.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:43.740
What is it?

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:44.430
R2.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:45.180
It's R2.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.800
Yeah, because we're
doing this in R2

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:48.330
so I can draw it on the board.

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:49.920
So it's actually everything.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.970
Because actually, every--
this is actually--

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.762
it says what are
the set of vectors

00:16:56.762 --> 00:16:59.220
for which when you take an
inner product with the 0 vector,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:00.720
you're bigger than
or equal to zero?

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.040
The answer is anything.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.844
So, in this case, you
would write it this way.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.990
This is R2, which is a cone.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.760
R2 is a cone.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:12.720
I mean, it's silly.

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:14.369
It's also subspace,
but it's cone.

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:15.300
Oh, by the way,
this is something

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.369
you're going to see that's
kind of interesting.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:23.099
When you have a dual cone,
if the cone itself is small--

00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:25.170
that's as small as cones get--

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.150
then your dual cone is big.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.550
And you've seen
things like this.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.770
This is like a
conjugacy type thing.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:36.090
So you have this in Fourier
transforms if you're--

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.780
I'll go into dialect,
if you don't mind.

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.110
But if you have a signal
that's very time-limited,

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:44.140
then its Fourier transform is
big ol' wide and conversely.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:46.210
The other thing about
conjugacy things

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.430
is you have this
general concept in math

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:52.630
that if you do it twice, you
get back to the original thing.

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:55.120
If you have complex numbers,
the conjugate of a conjugate

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:57.520
is the number again.

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:00.280
Over here with subspaces
like I was talking before,

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:03.820
if you have a subspace and
you go V perp, then perp that.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:05.680
Now, of course,
your head explodes.

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:09.250
Because if you think about what
V perp is, it's someone says,

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:10.510
what are you looking at?

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:12.820
You go, oh, it's the
set of all vectors that

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:15.610
are orthogonal to
all vectors that

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.120
are orthogonal to all
vectors in the subspace.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.690
Now, you can pretend you
understand what I just said.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:23.970
But I don't.

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:25.310
I didn't get it.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:26.710
And no human being could.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:28.960
I mean, well, maybe a
few, but they're weird.

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:32.650
And so I'm just
saying these things--

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.690
but you start using
powerful notation

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:36.520
and you get used
to these things.

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:41.430
So, what is the dual
cone of R2, by the way?

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:42.480
It's that.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:46.020
So the only vector that has
a non-negative inner product

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.720
with all vectors in R2 is zero.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.040
Hey, look at that.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:50.940
Funny about that.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.320
The conjugate, the dual
of the dual is itself.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.523
And these are just
like meta ideas

00:18:57.523 --> 00:18:59.940
that we're going to see in
different points in the course.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.060
Let me draw some non-silly ones.

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:05.860
How about this?

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:07.020
Here's a cone in R2.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:08.190
It's a ray.

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:12.840
It's just all-- it's all
non-negative multiples

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:13.408
of a vector.

00:19:13.408 --> 00:19:14.700
This just goes off to infinity.

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:16.740
That's a ray, it's called.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:18.510
What's the dual cone of it?

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:19.530
How do we find out?

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.990
How do we figure it out?

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:22.890
Yeah?

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:25.290
The half space that
it's orthogonal to.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:25.920
Yeah.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.345
So before we just jump in,
I mean, that's correct.

00:19:30.345 --> 00:19:32.350
But let's do the following.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:35.280
Let's first just ask
like whether a vector

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:36.730
is in the dual cone.

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:37.920
This is K right here.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:40.150
And I want to know
if you're in K star.

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.120
So, here's a vector.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:44.730
Is that in K star?

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:45.600
Yeah.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:46.380
It is, yeah.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.590
Yeah, because
basically, this thing

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.620
has a non-negative inner product
with everything on this ray.

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.710
I mean, on the ray,
it just scales it.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.200
OK, so that's in the dual cone.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.470
How about this guy over here?

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:00.345
How about that point?

00:20:00.345 --> 00:20:00.845
Yeah.

00:20:00.845 --> 00:20:01.470
Yeah.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:02.490
OK.

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:05.200
How about this point over here?

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:05.700
No.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:06.200
No.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:07.620
It doesn't work.

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.200
And so, basically, by
doing a couple of these,

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:12.930
you will quickly figure
out that, in fact, this

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:13.920
is the dual cone.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.100
It's a half space.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:19.200
So you could say something fancy
like, the dual cone of a ray--

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.750
the dual of a ray
is a half space.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:25.930
And notice that that has our
small, big concept embedded

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:26.430
there.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:30.420
A ray is kind of a very
small, little baby sharp cone.

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:35.070
And then a half space is
pretty much as blunt as a cone

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:35.970
as you can get.

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:40.590
Well, you can have all of
our tube, but that's silly.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.520
OK, everybody got this?

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:44.100
So I think these
are the pictures

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:47.643
you can talk to people you
work with or something.

00:20:47.643 --> 00:20:49.060
But some of these
get complicated.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:50.560
And actually, a lot
of these things,

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.370
you actually have to show.

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:55.370
I mean, and they're not
totally straightforward.

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:58.360
They involve Cauchy-Schwartz
inequality or something

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:01.190
like that.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.960
And these are probably best done
in either homework exercises

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:07.630
or something like that, but
the things you'd have to show.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:10.780
For example, let's
say K equals R

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.940
plus to the n,
non-negative orthant.

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.240
And when I say the dual of
that turns out to be-- well,

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:18.290
let's just do it in
R2, how about that?

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:21.280
We'll do it in R2
and then that'll

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:22.930
give you a visual
on it, and then

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:24.638
you'll know that it
actually generalizes.

00:21:24.638 --> 00:21:27.690
So, here's my cone K.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:31.990
And now I just ask questions
like, who's in the dual cone?

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.230
And I ask a question like,
is that in the dual cone?

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:38.530
No, because look, here's a
point over here in the cone.

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:40.780
And it's got a negative inner
product with this thing.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.790
So this is not.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:44.305
Is this in the dual cone?

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:47.790
Let's go slow.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:52.260
What I'm asking is,
does this vector

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.600
have a non-negative
inner product?

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.760
Is it is it plus
or minus 90-- is it

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:00.010
within 90 degrees of
every vector here?

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:01.380
And the answer is it is.

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:03.600
And what you figure
out pretty quickly

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.210
is that the dual
cone is going to be

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.920
this-- it's basically itself.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:12.210
And then that has a fancy name
that's called a self-dual cone.

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:13.110
So this is the--

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:15.570
now, by the way, I don't think
that you could get all of it

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:16.110
now.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:19.170
So, if you didn't get
it all, not a problem.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:24.120
You will have many, many
chances to get this later.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:25.770
Now, it turns out
there's connections

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.230
between minimum,
minimal, and dual cones

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:29.800
and things like that.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.350
And this is actually
going to end up

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:34.980
being super important in
a lot of practical stuff

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.210
that we'll see later.

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.490
Actually, a lot of
times you've already

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.270
seen it in other contexts.

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:46.960
So, if a set over here, that's
S, has a minimum point--

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.650
in this case, it's x--

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:54.100
then it's a minimum
element if and only

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:57.550
if whenever you minimize
lambda transpose z

00:22:57.550 --> 00:23:04.750
over S, for any lambda in the
dual cone, you get actually x--

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:08.467
it says x is the unique
minimizer of this.

00:23:08.467 --> 00:23:10.300
I mean, that's actually
kind of interesting.

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:14.590
Let's suppose that the cone
is the non-negative right.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:17.380
Then what this says is
actually really interesting.

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:22.180
If you are a minimum point
of a set with respect

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:27.580
to the non-negative orthant,
it says that pick any positive

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:28.450
weights--

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:31.900
that's lambda-- form this
linear combination, minimize,

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.380
and you always get
the same thing.

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:36.670
You always arrive at
this minimum element.

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:38.147
So that's what that means.

00:23:38.147 --> 00:23:39.730
It means independent
of these weights.

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:41.355
However you weight
them doesn't matter,

00:23:41.355 --> 00:23:44.410
you always get this minimum
out, which kind of makes sense.

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:48.340
Minimal elements, you can find
these actually very nicely.

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:51.100
This says you simply
take any weight that

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:54.070
is positive in the
dual cone and then

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:56.530
you minimize this
linear function over Z.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:59.380
And what happens then
is you are guaranteed

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:02.110
to get a minimal point.

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:04.100
Now you've seen this
in other contexts.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:07.300
This is like when you're doing
multi-criterion optimization

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:08.950
and you make a
weighted combination

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:11.410
and you minimize a non-negative
weighted combination,

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:15.730
you get something that's Pareto
optimal, which we'll talk

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:17.758
about all this stuff later.

00:24:17.758 --> 00:24:18.550
There's a question.

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:19.860
What is z?

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:20.762
What is what?

00:24:20.762 --> 00:24:23.017
What is z here?

00:24:23.017 --> 00:24:24.055
What is-- I'm sorry.

00:24:24.055 --> 00:24:24.730
Z.

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:26.290
V?

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:27.353
Sorry.

00:24:27.353 --> 00:24:28.100
Z.

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:28.600
Z?

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:29.100
Yeah.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:30.050
Is there something z?

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:31.730
Z is a dummy variable.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.760
So, if I say--

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:38.900
this is a function, which is f
of z equals lambda transpose z.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:40.850
So z is a dummy variable.

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:41.840
It has no meaning.

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:44.870
You can change it to
your favorite character.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:45.530
Yeah?

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:47.940
Can you explain
what lambda means?

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.800
Well, lambda is anything
that is positive

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:55.470
in the dual inequality.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.830
So it's any vector in the
positive dual inequality.

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:00.330
That's what it means.

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.760
So, again, this is weird
and out of context,

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:05.530
so I wouldn't worry
too much about it.

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:08.770
But it's something we will
see over and over again.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:09.610
OK.

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:14.470
And our first baby, childish
application from economics

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.450
goes like this.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:17.710
I mean, this is silly.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:22.210
So I have to produce a product,
and I have a production set.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:24.580
And a production
set in general tells

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:27.940
you-- and you have different
ways of producing it.

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:30.250
And a production set
in general tells you--

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:33.580
it tells you what are
all the choices that

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:37.630
parametrize your production
set, which is feasible.

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:40.420
And so this is just a made-up
thing with fuel and labor.

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:41.920
I mean, it's silly,
but there it is.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:43.810
And it said, we
can make-- we can

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:46.810
get away-- we can
produce what we need to

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:48.880
with any point in here.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:51.940
And I mean, we're getting a
little bit ahead of ourselves,

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:53.290
we could talk about this.

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:54.348
If we decide that--

00:25:54.348 --> 00:25:56.140
I mean, here, I'd call
them fuel and labor,

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:57.640
but it doesn't matter.

00:25:57.640 --> 00:26:02.020
If we decide that each of
these has value, positive value

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:03.880
not negative value,
then what that

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:08.540
tells you is everything down
and to the left is better.

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:12.890
Because, for example,
yeah, let's see.

00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:16.940
If you took this point and you
say, thank you for your order,

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:20.480
here's how I'm going
to run my business.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:21.778
And you take this point.

00:26:21.778 --> 00:26:23.195
Anybody have a
comment about that?

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:27.240
What?

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:28.540
Yeah, I heard the word.

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:29.040
It's good.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:30.220
We'll get to it later.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:31.770
It's not efficient.

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:33.390
Or I'll tell you,
the technical term

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.090
for that is that would
be a stupid choice.

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:37.770
That's the technical term.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:40.740
Because you'd say, why
would you run it like that?

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.870
Excuse me, but this is silly.

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.730
If you're going to do
that, why not do this?

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:47.010
And I can make the
exact same stuff

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:50.228
and I'll use less
fuel and less labor.

00:26:50.228 --> 00:26:52.770
And some would-- I don't know
what they would say about that.

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:54.780
And they'd say, yeah,
but how do it's better?

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:56.738
I haven't told you the
price of fuel and labor.

00:26:56.738 --> 00:26:58.950
And you go, dude, as
long as they're positive,

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:00.540
this is better.

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:01.570
Everybody got this?

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:03.270
So we'll get to all this.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:06.660
And you could see that these
are just concepts of efficiency

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:07.620
and minimal points.

00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:10.733
And one name for this
is Pareto optimal.

00:27:10.733 --> 00:27:12.150
And again, we'll
come back to this

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.353
later in quite a lot of detail.

00:27:14.353 --> 00:27:16.020
But this is things
you've seen, I think,

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.990
or things you've
heard of, maybe.

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:20.860
OK.

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:25.170
OK, now we're going to
jump into convex functions.

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:29.010
And first, I'll just say
what a convex function is.

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:34.200
So a convex function, it's
a function from Rn to R.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:35.250
It's got a domain.

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:38.640
That means that's all the
points for which f is defined.

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:41.190
That's the domain, OK?

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.690
And the domain has to be convex.

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:45.850
And then it has to
satisfy this inequality.

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:47.940
And what that
inequality says is--

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:49.905
I mean, there are
some weird terms,

00:27:49.905 --> 00:27:51.390
but I forget what
people call this.

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.930
But this is the graph
of the function here.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:56.970
Oh, this is called
a chord, I think.

00:27:56.970 --> 00:28:00.400
That's some ancient-- comes
from ancient Greek or something.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:01.890
So that's a chord
of the function.

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:05.370
And what it says is the
chord lies above the graph,

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:10.320
or I like to think of it, it
has non-negative curvature.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.300
It can have zero curvature.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.210
Then there would be
like an affine function.

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:18.360
But convexity means
non-negative curvature.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:19.870
Or you could even say this.

00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:22.200
You'd say all convexity
is we're talking

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:24.360
about the sine of the
second derivative.

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:25.200
We'll get to that.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.790
But roughly speaking,
that's what it means.

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:29.710
OK.

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:33.910
And we say a function is
concave if minus f is convex.

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:36.700
That means it curves down.

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:38.860
And a function is
strictly convex

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:42.040
if this inequality here holds
strictly as long as theta

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:44.470
is strictly between 0 and 1.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.870
So, you could think
of it lots of ways.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.620
This says f of the
mixture is less than

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:52.870
or equal to the mixture of f.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.360
So, it tells you
that when you compute

00:28:55.360 --> 00:29:00.460
a mixture and the function,
an inequality always holds.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:04.060
So that's a very good
way to think of it.

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:08.030
This is what a
convex function is.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:09.360
Let's look at some examples.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:12.200
First, we'll just look on
examples on R. By the way,

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.120
how do you check if a
function on R is convex?

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:16.700
You plot it in your
head, basically.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.060
I mean, at some point,
somebody would, I don't know.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:24.662
If you're being deposed by a
group of surly mathematicians,

00:29:24.662 --> 00:29:26.370
I suppose you'd actually
have to show it,

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:27.578
and you can't draw a picture.

00:29:27.578 --> 00:29:30.200
But the point is for an
R, you just visualize it,

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:31.280
and you can say.

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:35.810
So the graph of ax
plus b, it's a line.

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:38.840
And it's actually
true that if you

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:41.060
take the chord between any
points on a line, I mean,

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:41.480
it's silly.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:41.980
It's on it.

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:44.180
Actually, what happens
is in that case,

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:45.710
this holds with equality.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:49.400
OK.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:50.630
Here's one, exponential.

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:52.820
And that's with a positive
or negative thing.

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:56.660
And we'll soon see ways you
could actually prove this,

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.040
but it's kind of silly.

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:00.450
You just draw a picture
and you say, look here,

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:01.880
I'll just draw you
some functions.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:02.510
Here's one.

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:04.310
And here's another.

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:06.177
This is E to the x.

00:30:06.177 --> 00:30:07.260
I shouldn't go below zero.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:09.552
That's E to the minus 2x or
I don't know, I made it up.

00:30:09.552 --> 00:30:10.620
But it looks like that.

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:13.560
And they clearly have
non-negative curvature.

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:16.560
So those are examples.

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:17.490
Powers.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:21.270
Well, a power is bigger
than 1 work and then powers

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:22.620
that are negative work, too.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:25.950
Because a power that's
negative might look like this.

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:29.010
Here's 1 over x, and
it goes like that.

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:31.200
And then that's
going to be convex.

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:35.730
Let's see.

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:37.140
Negative entropy.

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:38.670
So x log x.

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:41.160
If I take a minus, that's
the negative entropy.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.625
And that is a function
that looks like this.

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:49.032
Here's zero.

00:30:49.032 --> 00:30:50.490
Here's one where
it's zero and then

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:54.030
it goes-- it's very steep there
and then goes up like that.

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.950
And it's convex.

00:30:55.950 --> 00:30:58.080
Or if you like, you could
think of it this way.

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:02.178
Entropy, which is minus
x log x, is concave.

00:31:02.178 --> 00:31:03.720
And these are the
kind of things when

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:06.450
we start doing some
statistics and other stuff,

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.210
you're going to-- this is going
to make-- it's going to make--

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:11.160
it's going to be very natural.

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:14.400
So these are just
simple examples.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:16.740
For concave, you'd
have the square root.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:20.400
There's your canonical
concave power.

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:22.650
But so is the 0.7 power.

00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:23.580
And the log.

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:29.040
That's another famous
one, is going to be log.

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:29.820
And don't worry.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.350
These are obvious,
but we will soon

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:34.490
get to ones that
are not obvious.

00:31:34.490 --> 00:31:36.800
OK.

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:40.320
Now we can have
some examples on Rn.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:42.950
So, if you have an affine
function-- so here,

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:45.020
the variable is a vector.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:46.860
And this is an affine function.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:51.860
And it turns out that's actually
both convex and concave.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:55.250
Because well, I mean,
I'd think of it this way.

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:58.130
Convexity means non-negative
curvature, concave means

00:31:58.130 --> 00:31:59.570
non-positive curvature.

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:03.230
If you're zero curvature,
otherwise known as affine,

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:08.420
then you're right on the
boundary, you satisfy both.

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:12.780
Any norm in Rn, any norm
at all, but for example,

00:32:12.780 --> 00:32:14.870
the pnorm would be 1.

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:20.040
And if you haven't seen the
pnorm before, it's time.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.820
And if you have, that's great.

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:24.380
I mean, certainly,
everyone here has

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:26.005
seen the 2-norm
or Euclidean norm,

00:32:26.005 --> 00:32:28.130
which is the square root
of the sum of the squares.

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:31.250
But you might not have heard
about the 3-norm, which

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.200
is the cube root of
the sum of the cubes

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:35.010
of the absolute values.

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:40.030
So you might ask like,
what do you use that for?

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:42.390
And the answer is I don't know.

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:44.970
But the 1-norm,
that's reasonable.

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.670
The 1-norm is the sum
of the absolute values.

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:51.210
That makes sense in a lot
of applications, actually.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:54.210
And then the infinity norm
is defined to be the maximum.

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:57.000
But the point is these
are convex functions.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:58.530
That's actually the point here.

00:32:58.530 --> 00:32:59.940
Somebody has to show that.

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:03.810
And I mean, we'll quickly
see ways to do it later

00:33:03.810 --> 00:33:05.670
that are quick and painless.

00:33:05.670 --> 00:33:09.930
But technically, somebody
has to show this.

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:10.710
OK.

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:14.200
Now we'll also look at
functions sometimes on matrices.

00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:18.000
So that means that the argument
of a function is a matrix.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:19.800
Like, for example,
the determinant

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:24.710
of a square matrix or the
trace of a matrix or something

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:25.210
like this.

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:27.002
So we're going to look
at things like that.

00:33:27.002 --> 00:33:30.810
Or actually, the
norm of a matrix.

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:33.120
And here, it's
interesting to know,

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:36.347
what does an affine function
on a matrix look like?

00:33:36.347 --> 00:33:38.430
Well, you know that an
affine function on a vector

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:39.310
looks like this.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:43.185
There exists a vector
a and a scalar b

00:33:43.185 --> 00:33:46.350
for which the function has
the form a transpose x plus b.

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.008
Well, the analog of
that for matrices

00:33:48.008 --> 00:33:49.300
is actually really interesting.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:49.980
It's this.

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:51.900
I mean, if you look at
this and you wrote out

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.720
the formula for that, you'd see
that they're kind of the same.

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.750
It's a sum, a weighted
sum of the coefficients,

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:00.930
that's a linear function,
plus a constant.

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:05.970
And you can write that
in fancy compact matrix

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:06.870
notation this way.

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:10.050
Trace of a matrix is the
sum of the diagonal entries.

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:14.010
And you can just check the
trace of a transpose x.

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:16.440
So when you see trace of
a transpose x from now on,

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:18.120
it's not some weird
matrix formula,

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:22.949
it's actually the inner
product between two matrices.

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:26.470
Should we just check that or--

00:34:26.470 --> 00:34:27.810
let's just check that.

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:34.810
So, if I have a matrix a and a
matrix b and I form this thing,

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:38.280
trace, what I have to
do is I have to add up

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:40.320
the entries on the diagonal.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.330
So let's look at the 1,
1 entry of this matrix.

00:34:43.330 --> 00:34:47.010
Well, the 1, 1 entry of
this matrix is going to be--

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:50.219
it's the row of a transpose
inner-producted with--

00:34:50.219 --> 00:34:51.630
if that's a verb--

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:53.940
inner-producted with-- it
doesn't sound good as a verb.

00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:54.440
Anyway.

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:56.025
With the first column of b.

00:34:56.025 --> 00:34:58.950
So, I'm going to write a is--

00:34:58.950 --> 00:35:00.960
well, I'll do that, yeah.

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:04.350
I'll write it out in its--

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.460
I'll leave the--
yeah, here, I'll

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:12.000
just make these both end
by end to make this simple.

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.360
So it looks like that.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:16.170
So, the 1, 1 entry
of this matrix

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:19.890
is actually a1 transpose b1.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:22.470
So that's what that is.

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:24.720
Yeah, because it's the first
row of a transpose, which

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:27.790
is the first column
of a inner-producted

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:30.070
with the first column of b.

00:35:30.070 --> 00:35:35.290
So, this thing is going
to be a1 transpose b1.

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:37.600
And then I'm going to add
up all the way to here,

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:39.760
and I'm going to get that.

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:41.860
And I could write
this out entry-wise.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:47.080
This is simply sum
over i and j, Aij, Bij.

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:51.507
And that sure looks like
an inner product to me.

00:35:51.507 --> 00:35:53.590
Or if you'd like to say--
another way people might

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:58.645
write it is you might write it's
vec of a transpose vec of b.

00:35:58.645 --> 00:36:02.002
This is not standard
mathematical notation,

00:36:02.002 --> 00:36:04.210
but most people would know
what you're talking about.

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:08.110
Vec is some mapping that strings
out the entries of your matrix

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:10.780
in some specified order.

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:14.440
Or even worse, you
could write this.

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:16.720
And I don't know what
that is, but it's

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:19.930
evocative of certain languages
I'm not even going to mention.

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:22.790
But everybody got that?

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:23.290
OK.

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:26.480
So these are just things
you'll see before.

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:30.050
So, here, here's a
function that's convex.

00:36:30.050 --> 00:36:31.430
Actually, an enormous convex.

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:33.347
It follows just from the
properties of a norm.

00:36:33.347 --> 00:36:36.140
But the spectral norm
or otherwise called

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:38.930
the 2-norm, induced 2-norm.

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:39.830
And it's this.

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:42.450
It's the maximum singular
value of that matrix.

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:45.668
That's a convex function.

00:36:45.668 --> 00:36:46.960
Yeah, by the way, it's really--

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:49.335
I mean, you can't write this
out analytically if if one--

00:36:49.335 --> 00:36:51.400
if any dimension of
x is bigger than 5,

00:36:51.400 --> 00:36:53.530
you cannot write out
a formula for it.

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:57.010
Because you'd have to
find determinant of x

00:36:57.010 --> 00:36:59.950
transpose x minus
lambda i that's

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:02.200
a polynomial of
degree at least five.

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:04.900
And there's no formula
for the roots of that.

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:07.300
Nevertheless, that
doesn't stop people

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:10.300
from using the
2-norm all the time.

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:12.790
But the point is that
function is really complicated

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:14.800
and it's convex.

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:17.930
So, you'll see some
others pretty soon.

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:19.960
OK.

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:24.220
So here's a general method.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.950
It's useful sometimes
for checking convexity.

00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:28.330
It says the following.

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:31.890
If I have a convex function and
I restrict it to a line-- now,

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:34.390
restricting it to a line means
I'm only going to look at its

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:38.590
values along the
line, a line in Rn--

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:40.713
then the restricted
function is convex.

00:37:40.713 --> 00:37:42.880
Not only that, it's a
function of a single variable,

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:46.340
you could plot it and just
look at it with your eyeballs

00:37:46.340 --> 00:37:48.440
and see that it goes up.

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:51.770
So by the way, this gives you
a completely reasonable way

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:54.530
to check if a
function is convex.

00:37:54.530 --> 00:37:56.210
If the methods
we're going to talk

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:59.780
about later today
and next lecture

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:05.510
don't work, then real simple,
you generate random lines

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:07.940
and then you plot the
function restricted.

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:13.032
And if every time that comes
up, it's got positive curvature,

00:38:13.032 --> 00:38:14.990
you hit carriage return
and go to the next one.

00:38:14.990 --> 00:38:17.070
I mean, this is not a
very good way to do it.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:18.650
But if you ever
see anything where

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:21.740
it's got even the
slightest non-convexity,

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:25.230
then you stop and you announce
that function is not convex.

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:25.730
OK.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:28.670
So, basically, it says,
it's convex if and only

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.710
if it is convex when
restricted to any line.

00:38:30.710 --> 00:38:32.430
And here's an example of this.

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:34.250
This is a cool example.

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:36.860
It has been known for
more than 100 years

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:39.980
that the log of the determinant
of a positive definite matrix

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:43.300
is concave, which is crazy.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:46.360
And it's really complicated
function if you think about it.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:49.480
It's the log of some
crazy polynomial

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:51.130
with n factorial terms in it.

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:52.510
Everybody following this?

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:55.630
It's just like, how on earth
would you know what that-- now,

00:38:55.630 --> 00:38:57.190
of course, we don't
compute the log

00:38:57.190 --> 00:38:58.510
of the determinant that way.

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:00.220
So that's silly.

00:39:00.220 --> 00:39:02.650
By the way, how do we compute
the log of the determinant?

00:39:02.650 --> 00:39:03.640
I mean, this is--

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:05.480
we'll talk about numerical
stuff later in the class,

00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:08.063
but how do you compute the log
of the determinant of a matrix?

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.280
I'm open to suggestions.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:16.020
Write out what the
determinant is?

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:20.020
No, that has n
factorial terms in it.

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:22.637
So how do you do this?

00:39:22.637 --> 00:39:23.970
You can compute the eigenvalues.

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:24.670
Eigenvalues.

00:39:24.670 --> 00:39:25.210
Yeah, sure.

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:28.270
OK, you can compute its
eigenvalues and then

00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:30.550
some the log of the eigenvalues.

00:39:30.550 --> 00:39:34.060
And suppose you wanted to exact
method that didn't involve

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:35.885
eigenvalues, what would you do?

00:39:35.885 --> 00:39:36.760
This is just for fun.

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.135
I mean, we'll talk about these
things later in the class.

00:39:39.135 --> 00:39:41.590
You don't have to know it now.

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:43.540
You'd take a Cholesky
factorization

00:39:43.540 --> 00:39:45.280
of the matrix and
then you'd take

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:46.900
the product of the entries--

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:49.510
well, you'd take the sum
of the log of the entries

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:51.130
on the diagonal.

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:53.830
And that's an order n
cubed operation as opposed

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:55.210
to n factorial.

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:57.370
And just for the record,
n cubed is a lot better

00:39:57.370 --> 00:39:58.540
than n factorial.

00:39:58.540 --> 00:39:59.830
OK.

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:01.990
That was an aside, didn't
have to do with anything.

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:04.490
so, let's see that-- so this
is actually saying something

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:04.990
that's--

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:07.420
I mean, you have to get
some respect for this.

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:10.390
That's a really
complicated function.

00:40:10.390 --> 00:40:12.100
And yet, it's concave.

00:40:12.100 --> 00:40:17.020
Not only that, they
knew this in 1905.

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:20.260
In fact, I think the
term convex had only

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:23.800
come in 10 or 15 years
ago, like at all.

00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:24.760
OK.

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:27.280
So here's how you'd show that.

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:31.600
So, to show this, what
I'll do-- now by the way,

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:34.750
if you knew information
theory, you could show this.

00:40:34.750 --> 00:40:37.420
Because you'd say, well,
I mean that's the entropy

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:41.300
of a Gaussian variable
or something like that.

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:42.943
And then you'd take
two things, it'd

00:40:42.943 --> 00:40:44.360
be a mixture of
Gaussian variables

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:46.550
and there'd be some
information theory

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:50.210
inequality, famous inequality,
that would tell you this.

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:54.140
But that's weird and
complicated for normal people.

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:57.590
So here's how you would--
here's how you'd do this.

00:40:57.590 --> 00:40:59.300
You take a matrix here.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:01.460
I'm going to pick an
arbitrary point X, which

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:03.595
is going to be capitalized
because it's a matrix.

00:41:03.595 --> 00:41:04.970
That's going to
be in the domain.

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:06.680
And then an arbitrary
direction V,

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:11.330
which is symmetric but not
necessarily positive definite.

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:14.930
So this, if I take X
plus tV, actually that

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:18.950
describes a line in
symmetric matrix space.

00:41:18.950 --> 00:41:20.240
Do you agree?

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:22.560
I mean, we can pretend
to visualize it,

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:23.930
but that's what it is.

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:26.960
So there's a line of
covariance matrices

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:30.380
or however you want
to think of it.

00:41:30.380 --> 00:41:34.640
And so we want to find out, is
this convex with respect to t?

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:37.407
Well, we can plot it.

00:41:37.407 --> 00:41:38.240
And that'll come up.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:38.782
It's correct.

00:41:38.782 --> 00:41:39.380
It's concave.

00:41:39.380 --> 00:41:40.350
Sorry, it's concave.

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:41.900
And so the way you'd
do this is here,

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:44.090
I just take this
matrix expression.

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:49.310
I take X to the 1/2 outside--

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:52.940
X to the minus 1/2 outside here,
I'd pull it out both sides.

00:41:52.940 --> 00:41:54.380
This becomes an i.

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:59.060
This becomes t times X to the
minus 1/2 VX to the minus 1/2.

00:41:59.060 --> 00:41:59.900
That's this thing.

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:01.170
So, I get this.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.530
Then the two other
terms, I get two terms

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:06.410
which look like log debt x
to the 1/2, each of which

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:12.590
is 1/2 log debt X. So I end
up with this expression here.

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:16.050
Now, I go to
eigenvalues and I say,

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:20.390
well, I can write this
thing out if I knew

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:22.520
the eigenvalues of this matrix.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:25.700
And if lambda are the
eigenvalues of this matrix,

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:28.850
this matrix is symmetric, but
it is not necessarily positive

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:31.580
semidefinite.

00:42:31.580 --> 00:42:34.305
So anyway, I end up
with an expression

00:42:34.305 --> 00:42:35.180
that looks like this.

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:36.560
This is as a function of t.

00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:41.030
Now we're cool because each--

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:42.650
we could quickly
convince ourselves

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:48.000
that log of 1 plus
t lambda is concave.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:51.417
No matter what lambda
is, it's concave.

00:42:51.417 --> 00:42:53.250
And then this is a sum
of concave functions,

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:54.000
we'll get to that.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:55.170
That's going to be concave.

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:56.730
And that's how you do it.

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:59.190
So, that would be that.

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:03.060
But this is already a not
totally obvious thing.

00:43:03.060 --> 00:43:04.770
And in fact, I guess
in a lot of classes,

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:09.772
people would end up showing this
by some very long, convoluted

00:43:09.772 --> 00:43:10.980
thing or something like that.

00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:13.560
It's fine.

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:15.390
OK, one more thing
we want to talk about

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:18.240
with convex functions
is they actually

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:19.770
extend very gracefully.

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.910
There's a way to handle
the domain very gracefully.

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:25.830
And the way that works is this.

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:28.843
What we do is you have
a convex function f.

00:43:28.843 --> 00:43:31.260
And what we do-- and let's
just talk about f for a minute.

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:33.210
So I have a convex function f.

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:35.670
If I call--

00:43:35.670 --> 00:43:40.655
I'm going to use the dialect of
maybe street-fighting computer

00:43:40.655 --> 00:43:41.530
science or something.

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:45.340
But if I call f with an
argument x that's in its domain,

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:47.470
I get a number like minus 11.

00:43:47.470 --> 00:43:49.240
And that's what it is.

00:43:49.240 --> 00:43:50.170
That's cool.

00:43:50.170 --> 00:43:54.230
If I call f at a point
outside the domain,

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:56.680
well, it depends on who
implemented the function.

00:43:56.680 --> 00:44:00.380
But maybe everything dumps
core or something like that.

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:02.050
But what should
happen is you should

00:44:02.050 --> 00:44:06.695
get a return value that's OOD,
odd, which means out of domain.

00:44:06.695 --> 00:44:08.570
By the way, if you call
it a couple of times,

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:13.900
you should get OOD and then a
nasty message, which is like,

00:44:13.900 --> 00:44:14.560
watch out.

00:44:14.560 --> 00:44:18.460
If you do that 10 more times and
your privileges for evaluating

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:19.990
this function are revoked.

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:21.460
That's how that should work.

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:25.360
So you should get something
like out of domain or NA or NAN

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:26.680
or whatever you like.

00:44:26.680 --> 00:44:28.030
OK.

00:44:28.030 --> 00:44:30.310
So it turns out,
what you do instead

00:44:30.310 --> 00:44:32.643
is I'm going to define
a new function f tilde.

00:44:32.643 --> 00:44:34.310
It's going to be
defined for everything.

00:44:34.310 --> 00:44:37.450
And if you're out of
the domain, you just

00:44:37.450 --> 00:44:39.240
call yourself infinity.

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:40.020
That's it.

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:41.970
And actually, what
you're going to find out

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:44.550
is that inequality works,
you can figure that out

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:46.050
in your head.

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:49.380
And everything just
works with this.

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:50.910
And actually, it's
nice because it

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:53.370
means a lot of times we'll
work with functions that can

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:54.990
have values of plus infinity.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:56.560
And those are just like--

00:44:56.560 --> 00:45:01.230
that's a way we'll see
to express constraints.

00:45:01.230 --> 00:45:02.970
That's how that's going to work.

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:04.680
OK.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:09.930
So, I mean, it's just
bookkeeping, but it does work.

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:13.110
I don't think whatever
IEEE 488 thing,

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:14.550
I don't think it supports this.

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:16.200
Actually, I should check if it--

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:17.370
I mean, it actually does.

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:19.230
They do have things
like plus inf and minus

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:20.670
infinity and stuff like that.

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:22.315
Whether or not people
when they write

00:45:22.315 --> 00:45:24.690
stuff for convex functions
actually support those things,

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:25.607
it's another question.

00:45:25.607 --> 00:45:27.630
I don't really know.

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:30.850
All right, so that's the
extended value extension.

00:45:30.850 --> 00:45:31.360
OK.

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:34.038
Now we get to some conditions.

00:45:34.038 --> 00:45:35.830
These are the traditional
ones, and they're

00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:40.720
based on tradition, which
is our multihundred year

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:42.340
tradition of calculus.

00:45:42.340 --> 00:45:45.070
And so it's actually
super interesting.

00:45:45.070 --> 00:45:46.810
The gradient of a
function-- by the way,

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:48.670
this construction
here, a lot of people

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:51.490
use different notation,
which I despise.

00:45:51.490 --> 00:45:52.090
I don't.

00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:54.010
I use just one fixed notation.

00:45:54.010 --> 00:45:58.460
And to write a vector, you
have exactly two choices.

00:45:58.460 --> 00:46:01.180
You can write it like this.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:05.110
A, B, C that's a vertical stack.

00:46:05.110 --> 00:46:06.445
Or you can write it this way.

00:46:09.490 --> 00:46:13.270
So, for me, the
parentheses and the comma

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:14.860
is the constructor for a vector.

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:15.412
Yeah?

00:46:15.412 --> 00:46:16.870
Is our convention
that the gradient

00:46:16.870 --> 00:46:17.860
is a column vector or--

00:46:17.860 --> 00:46:18.280
That's correct.

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:19.090
That's a column vector.

00:46:19.090 --> 00:46:19.750
That's right.

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:20.980
That's exactly right.

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:22.060
Right, right.

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:27.245
OK, so that's the gradient.

00:46:27.245 --> 00:46:28.870
By the way, people
write all sorts of--

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:30.700
I mean, the problem is
people are programming

00:46:30.700 --> 00:46:32.325
in lots of different
languages and they

00:46:32.325 --> 00:46:34.360
start writing things like this.

00:46:34.360 --> 00:46:38.542
And they start
writing, I don't know.

00:46:38.542 --> 00:46:40.750
And then they start writing
all sorts of weird stuff.

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:44.590
I understand what they're
saying, but I'd judge them.

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:46.780
So I just make a little note.

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:49.210
And even worse, in the
same paper or talk,

00:46:49.210 --> 00:46:50.320
you'll find these two.

00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:53.950
And then they really get judged.

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:57.280
OK, so that was just a
quick notation comment.

00:46:57.280 --> 00:46:59.110
So the gradient
is just basically

00:46:59.110 --> 00:47:01.180
the set of all
partial derivatives.

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:08.470
And you should recognize this
as the first order Taylor

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:13.530
approximation of f
developed at the point x.

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:16.560
And what that means is this
function here is affine.

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:17.280
It is.

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:19.320
It's linear plus a
constant, number one.

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:24.000
And number two, at the point
x, it's actually correct.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:26.250
This thing, which
you might call f hat,

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:31.560
is actually equal to f when
you plug in y equals x.

00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:32.560
That goes away.

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:34.140
And that's just f of x.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:36.990
So, what calculus
tells you is this.

00:47:36.990 --> 00:47:39.420
It says that this
function here is

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:42.990
really close to this
function if y is close to x.

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:47.700
And when I say really close,
I mean like close squared.

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:49.980
That's what I meant
when I said that.

00:47:49.980 --> 00:47:52.980
So, it wasn't just the
English intensifier.

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:54.420
OK.

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:57.300
So what this says is
actually super interesting.

00:47:57.300 --> 00:47:59.310
And this picture actually
is related basically

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:02.470
to the entire course.

00:48:02.470 --> 00:48:05.500
What this says is that
the first order Taylor

00:48:05.500 --> 00:48:13.200
expansion for a convex function,
it's from calculus in 1770.

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:14.160
We knew.

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:15.270
I mean, if the notation
would be different,

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:16.687
we'd probably be
writing in Latin.

00:48:16.687 --> 00:48:23.785
But we knew that the Taylor
approximation was really close

00:48:23.785 --> 00:48:25.410
if you're nearby the
point at which you

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:27.060
develop the expansion.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:29.580
That's what you know in 1770.

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:31.810
But what we know is
actually really cool.

00:48:31.810 --> 00:48:36.930
It says that basically,
it is a global lower bound

00:48:36.930 --> 00:48:39.270
or underestimator.

00:48:39.270 --> 00:48:41.550
So that's what it tells you.

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:44.820
And I think this picture, if you
have this picture in your mind,

00:48:44.820 --> 00:48:47.460
you're good to go.

00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:50.910
And in fact, in some weird
way, all the weird stuff

00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:53.080
in the class comes down
to this one picture,

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:54.750
which is really dumb.

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:55.757
So what it says--

00:48:55.757 --> 00:48:57.840
so if you think about
calculus, calculus gives you

00:48:57.840 --> 00:48:59.610
a-- it's a local statement.

00:48:59.610 --> 00:49:02.490
It says this approximation
is really close to this thing

00:49:02.490 --> 00:49:03.618
if you're nearby.

00:49:03.618 --> 00:49:05.160
And then you start
saying like, well,

00:49:05.160 --> 00:49:06.060
how close do you need to be?

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:07.990
And you're like, oh, that
depends and blah, blah, blah,

00:49:07.990 --> 00:49:08.490
OK.

00:49:08.490 --> 00:49:11.350
And it gets
complicated and vague.

00:49:11.350 --> 00:49:13.180
That's calculus.

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:14.500
This is different.

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:18.100
This says, no, we have
just a global lower bound.

00:49:18.100 --> 00:49:19.330
It's always that way.

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:20.270
And so actually, this
is going to come--

00:49:20.270 --> 00:49:22.657
because I remember once I was
talking to somebody about--

00:49:22.657 --> 00:49:23.740
OK, it was circuit design.

00:49:23.740 --> 00:49:25.073
We were talking about something.

00:49:25.073 --> 00:49:29.180
And I said, well, I mean,
that's the best circuit design.

00:49:29.180 --> 00:49:32.590
And the person looked at me
and he said, how could you

00:49:32.590 --> 00:49:34.150
possibly know that?

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:35.710
It's ridiculous.

00:49:35.710 --> 00:49:37.960
How could you
possibly know that?

00:49:37.960 --> 00:49:41.902
What kind of hubris-- what
would make you say that?

00:49:41.902 --> 00:49:43.360
I was like, because
it's true, man.

00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:45.830
It's just true.

00:49:45.830 --> 00:49:49.060
And then he looked at
me and he said, why?

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:51.010
How could you say that?

00:49:51.010 --> 00:49:53.110
And actually, it just
comes down to this.

00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:56.080
So what it says is that
when you have convexity,

00:49:56.080 --> 00:49:58.330
you can make global statements.

00:49:58.330 --> 00:50:01.120
In calculus, you make
local statements.

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:03.850
If I'm minimizing a
function and the gradient

00:50:03.850 --> 00:50:07.240
is zero or something like
that, I can say, yeah, totally,

00:50:07.240 --> 00:50:09.530
if you move a small
amount in any direction,

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:11.780
the function is going to go
up or something like that.

00:50:11.780 --> 00:50:12.898
You see what I'm saying?

00:50:12.898 --> 00:50:14.440
But you have no idea
that there's not

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:16.720
some place, some other
neighborhood you haven't ever

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:19.810
seen before, where the
function is smaller.

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:22.588
For convex functions,
that can't happen.

00:50:22.588 --> 00:50:23.380
Everybody got this?

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:28.000
So roughly speaking, it says
that from local information,

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:31.630
you actually get
global inequalities.

00:50:31.630 --> 00:50:33.880
I know it sounds like I'm
making a big deal out of it,

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:34.450
and it is nothing.

00:50:34.450 --> 00:50:35.937
But to be perfectly
honest, this is

00:50:35.937 --> 00:50:38.020
the entire-- this is the
point in the whole class.

00:50:38.020 --> 00:50:41.678
So, I mean, not that you care
about this, but you will later.

00:50:41.678 --> 00:50:42.220
I don't know.

00:50:42.220 --> 00:50:43.803
By, whatever, by the
sixth week you'll

00:50:43.803 --> 00:50:46.210
just say, you'll be solving
problems and saying,

00:50:46.210 --> 00:50:48.045
yeah, that's the best.

00:50:48.045 --> 00:50:49.420
And you don't even
think about it

00:50:49.420 --> 00:50:52.003
because you'll have optimized a
problem with a couple thousand

00:50:52.003 --> 00:50:52.570
variables.

00:50:52.570 --> 00:50:53.860
And I got news for you.

00:50:53.860 --> 00:50:56.500
R couple thousand is
a really big place.

00:50:56.500 --> 00:50:59.020
And that's a very strong
statement when you say,

00:50:59.020 --> 00:51:01.427
yeah, I just minimized it in
the optimal objective 3.1.

00:51:01.427 --> 00:51:03.010
And someone says,
what does that mean?

00:51:03.010 --> 00:51:06.340
You go, it means any other
x you pick that's feasible,

00:51:06.340 --> 00:51:08.800
it's going to have an objective
function greater than 3.1.

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:09.790
That's what's you're saying.

00:51:09.790 --> 00:51:12.070
And if you think about it,
that's a crazy statement.

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:14.050
And someone say like,
how could you say that?

00:51:14.050 --> 00:51:16.100
Anyway, it's just
because of this.

00:51:16.100 --> 00:51:17.980
You could mumble
something about, well,

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:22.000
for a convex functions,
from local statements,

00:51:22.000 --> 00:51:25.387
we can actually assert
global things about--

00:51:25.387 --> 00:51:27.970
OK, OK, I'll stop because it's
just getting weird and ranting.

00:51:27.970 --> 00:51:31.480
OK, so we'll just move on.

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:33.730
Next topic.

00:51:33.730 --> 00:51:35.680
Second order conditions.

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:38.800
So, if a function is
twice differentiable,

00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:41.180
then its Hessian is
a symmetric matrix,

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:45.490
which is the set of all
partial derivatives.

00:51:45.490 --> 00:51:49.210
And it turns out,
and this makes--

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:51.070
these are very simple
things to show.

00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:53.290
It turns out a function
is convex if and only

00:51:53.290 --> 00:51:55.750
if assuming it's
twice differentiable,

00:51:55.750 --> 00:52:00.820
assuming that then its
Hessian has to be positive

00:52:00.820 --> 00:52:03.430
semidefinite at all points.

00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:06.610
And this makes total sense.

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:09.670
Roughly speaking,
if n was 1, you'd

00:52:09.670 --> 00:52:11.530
say, if a function
is differentiable,

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:14.380
f prime prime is non-negative.

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:16.780
So that's how you could--
now you could do things

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:18.640
like someone walks up
the street and says,

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:24.967
show me that the entropy
minus x log x is concave.

00:52:24.967 --> 00:52:27.550
So you'd differentiate it twice
and you get something and say,

00:52:27.550 --> 00:52:29.140
there, see?

00:52:29.140 --> 00:52:31.180
It's always non-positive.

00:52:31.180 --> 00:52:34.060
And you say, therefore,
it's-- so everybody got that?

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.580
So this is OK.

00:52:36.580 --> 00:52:37.450
Yeah.

00:52:37.450 --> 00:52:39.850
OK, so this is the idea
of-- and it kind of fits.

00:52:39.850 --> 00:52:40.840
It makes perfect sense.

00:52:40.840 --> 00:52:42.673
Oh, and by the way, the
Hessian is a measure

00:52:42.673 --> 00:52:45.010
of the curvature of a function.

00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:46.840
So, it's cool.

00:52:46.840 --> 00:52:49.760
It tells you--
it's kind of cool.

00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:51.870
It tells you that--

00:52:51.870 --> 00:52:55.150
I mean, if I give a
matrix or whatever,

00:52:55.150 --> 00:52:58.000
you could be pedantic and call
it a 2-tensor or something

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:00.010
and someone says,
well, what do you

00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:02.190
mean the function curves up?

00:53:02.190 --> 00:53:04.190
And you mean, oh, its
curvature is non-negative.

00:53:04.190 --> 00:53:06.970
And if you had to make something
up about what that means,

00:53:06.970 --> 00:53:08.500
it means that.

00:53:08.500 --> 00:53:10.930
So that's the picture.

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:14.920
Again, these are actually
really easy to show.

00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:17.020
So, I'm not going to do that.

00:53:17.020 --> 00:53:19.210
But it's a good
way to just start

00:53:19.210 --> 00:53:20.800
thinking about these things.

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:21.550
OK.

00:53:21.550 --> 00:53:24.200
So let's look at some examples.

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:26.540
The first one, here is
a quadratic function.

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:30.280
So here, I have a symmetric--
this is a quadratic form.

00:53:30.280 --> 00:53:33.880
It's x transpose Px, where
P is symmetric matrix.

00:53:33.880 --> 00:53:35.290
By the way, P is not symmetric.

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:37.570
I can replace it with
its symmetric part, which

00:53:37.570 --> 00:53:41.380
is P plus P transposed divided
by 2, and now it's symmetric.

00:53:41.380 --> 00:53:43.210
And it doesn't
change this function.

00:53:43.210 --> 00:53:44.590
So that's a quadratic form.

00:53:44.590 --> 00:53:50.050
If you add to a quadratic form
a linear term and a constant,

00:53:50.050 --> 00:53:52.240
you get a quadratic function.

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:57.010
So, this is the
analog of basically P

00:53:57.010 --> 00:54:01.870
over 2x squared plus qx
plus R for in dimension one.

00:54:01.870 --> 00:54:03.250
So that's what this is.

00:54:03.250 --> 00:54:06.160
Well, the gradient turns
out to be Px plus q.

00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:09.040
And then it turns out
that the Hessian is just

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:09.880
P. It's constant.

00:54:09.880 --> 00:54:11.050
It has constant curvature.

00:54:11.050 --> 00:54:12.058
So you'd say, well--

00:54:12.058 --> 00:54:12.850
I mean, it's silly.

00:54:12.850 --> 00:54:15.850
You'd say, oh, a
quadratic function

00:54:15.850 --> 00:54:17.000
has constant curvature.

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:19.660
Well, duh, that's what it means.

00:54:19.660 --> 00:54:22.600
And then it turns
out if this matrix

00:54:22.600 --> 00:54:25.330
P is positive semidefinite,
this thing is convex.

00:54:25.330 --> 00:54:28.180
And that's if and only if.

00:54:28.180 --> 00:54:28.870
Here's one.

00:54:28.870 --> 00:54:30.460
Least squares objective.

00:54:30.460 --> 00:54:34.285
So here, I have a matrix A.
Doesn't matter its dimensions,

00:54:34.285 --> 00:54:36.160
except it has to be--
this has to make sense.

00:54:36.160 --> 00:54:37.790
And a vector b.

00:54:37.790 --> 00:54:39.380
I form this thing.

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:43.660
And if I expand it out,
the gradient is this thing.

00:54:43.660 --> 00:54:46.750
This is what you see in the
normal equations so-called.

00:54:46.750 --> 00:54:49.930
And then the Hessian
is 2A transpose A,

00:54:49.930 --> 00:54:51.040
positive semidefinite.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:53.080
So it's done.

00:54:53.080 --> 00:54:54.940
So these are just
good things to know.

00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:56.540
Now we get to a fun one.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:55:00.670
So this is the simplest
convex function

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:03.457
that most people are
utterly unaware of.

00:55:03.457 --> 00:55:04.540
If I walk up to a person--

00:55:04.540 --> 00:55:05.680
OK, it depends on
the neighborhood.

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:07.805
But if I walk up to a person
on the street and say,

00:55:07.805 --> 00:55:08.980
hey, is x convex?

00:55:08.980 --> 00:55:11.140
They're like, duh,
of course, it is.

00:55:11.140 --> 00:55:14.680
I mean, because you just draw
the picture in your head.

00:55:14.680 --> 00:55:17.380
People would know that a
norm is convex because it's

00:55:17.380 --> 00:55:18.940
like the triangle inequality.

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:20.830
These are obvious.

00:55:20.830 --> 00:55:22.390
Here's a super simple one.

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:24.010
The function x squared over y.

00:55:24.010 --> 00:55:27.180
Two variables, y is positive.

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:29.790
So that's it.

00:55:29.790 --> 00:55:33.390
And it's not obvious that--

00:55:33.390 --> 00:55:35.650
oh, there are some
obvious things here.

00:55:35.650 --> 00:55:38.610
If I fix y, it's convex
in x because it's

00:55:38.610 --> 00:55:40.810
a positive multiple
of x squared.

00:55:40.810 --> 00:55:47.400
If I fix x, it's convex in
y because it's a constant 1

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:49.470
over y, and it looks like this.

00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:52.050
So, it's convex.

00:55:52.050 --> 00:55:53.880
Much more, this
says it's actually

00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:57.420
jointly convex in x and y.

00:55:57.420 --> 00:55:58.650
And you can check.

00:55:58.650 --> 00:56:00.030
I mean, but here's a quick one.

00:56:00.030 --> 00:56:05.610
I worked out the Hessian for you
and expressed it as a rank one

00:56:05.610 --> 00:56:07.320
positive semidefinite matrix.

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:09.365
That's what this is.

00:56:09.365 --> 00:56:11.490
By the way, the interesting
part about the function

00:56:11.490 --> 00:56:13.740
is because it has
rank one, that means

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:15.630
that wherever you
are on the surface,

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:17.700
it's actually got one
direction where it's flat

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:18.930
and one where it's curving.

00:56:18.930 --> 00:56:20.612
But there was a question.

00:56:20.612 --> 00:56:25.050
[INAUDIBLE] So if a
function is jointly convex,

00:56:25.050 --> 00:56:28.460
does that mean the individual
variables are also convex?

00:56:28.460 --> 00:56:29.960
Yes.

00:56:29.960 --> 00:56:32.648
But let me be very
careful to make sure--

00:56:32.648 --> 00:56:34.190
so there is something
in there that's

00:56:34.190 --> 00:56:35.460
false and very important.

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:36.560
So let's get to that.

00:56:36.560 --> 00:56:41.180
If a function of two
variables is convex,

00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:43.700
it is definitely convex,
in fact, by our thing,

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:45.830
by restricting it to a line.

00:56:45.830 --> 00:56:48.680
One line is just that the first
variable changes and the second

00:56:48.680 --> 00:56:50.120
doesn't.

00:56:50.120 --> 00:56:52.220
So yes, if a function
of two variables

00:56:52.220 --> 00:56:55.400
is convex, then if
you fix one variable,

00:56:55.400 --> 00:56:58.310
it's convex in the other
one, and vice versa.

00:56:58.310 --> 00:57:01.140
The converse of that
statement is false.

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:07.130
It is not true-- a function
can be convex in each variable

00:57:07.130 --> 00:57:09.710
when the other is fixed.

00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:13.333
Somebody want to
give me an example?

00:57:13.333 --> 00:57:13.875
Like, simple?

00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:20.640
Maybe a saddle.

00:57:20.640 --> 00:57:21.500
OK, a saddle.

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:22.170
Yeah, here.

00:57:22.170 --> 00:57:22.670
OK.

00:57:22.670 --> 00:57:24.510
Yeah, that'll do the trick.

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:27.795
But let me-- it depends
which directions

00:57:27.795 --> 00:57:28.670
you move in a saddle.

00:57:28.670 --> 00:57:29.330
But roughly right.

00:57:29.330 --> 00:57:29.780
Here's one.

00:57:29.780 --> 00:57:30.080
Ready?

00:57:30.080 --> 00:57:31.538
Here's a function
of two variables.

00:57:31.538 --> 00:57:33.470
Ready?

00:57:33.470 --> 00:57:35.180
That's the product.

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:36.920
That's a quadratic form.

00:57:36.920 --> 00:57:39.200
This is equal to--

00:57:39.200 --> 00:57:40.400
well, OK.

00:57:40.400 --> 00:57:45.950
That's equal to x, y transpose
times maybe 1/2 and 1, 1, 0, 0.

00:57:45.950 --> 00:57:48.110
Everybody agree with that?

00:57:48.110 --> 00:57:50.600
OK, so that's my function.

00:57:50.600 --> 00:57:53.600
Now, in your mind,
please fix y and tell me

00:57:53.600 --> 00:57:57.080
if this is convex in x.

00:57:57.080 --> 00:57:58.190
It's linear.

00:57:58.190 --> 00:58:01.220
Now in your mind, fix
x, please, and tell me

00:58:01.220 --> 00:58:03.980
what is the curvature in y.

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:04.850
It's linear.

00:58:04.850 --> 00:58:05.510
So it's convex.

00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:07.280
It's both convex and concave.

00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:09.440
Is the function convex?

00:58:09.440 --> 00:58:10.970
Well, its graph is a saddle.

00:58:10.970 --> 00:58:12.350
I think you'd suggested that.

00:58:12.350 --> 00:58:15.090
So that's how you know it's not.

00:58:15.090 --> 00:58:17.900
But the other thing is, well,
wait, we just looked at this.

00:58:17.900 --> 00:58:19.880
I mean, the question
is whether this matrix

00:58:19.880 --> 00:58:21.290
is positive semidefinite.

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:23.210
Is that matrix
positive semidefinite?

00:58:27.780 --> 00:58:29.100
No.

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:34.980
I sense that our intuition about
positive semidefinite matrices

00:58:34.980 --> 00:58:37.500
is not well enough formed.

00:58:37.500 --> 00:58:39.750
I just have a sense here.

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:42.510
How would I look at this
and know that that's not

00:58:42.510 --> 00:58:44.890
positive semidefinite?

00:58:44.890 --> 00:58:47.380
Why?

00:58:47.380 --> 00:58:49.557
Yeah, OK we can do eigenvalues.

00:58:49.557 --> 00:58:51.890
I mean, I happen to know the
eigenvalues of that matrix.

00:58:51.890 --> 00:58:52.630
Do you?

00:58:52.630 --> 00:58:53.680
Minus 1 and 1.

00:58:53.680 --> 00:58:54.880
OK, minus 1 and 1.

00:58:54.880 --> 00:58:56.440
And it's game over with minus 1.

00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:57.880
There was a
suggestion back there.

00:58:57.880 --> 00:59:00.400
I actually can't read that
matrix from back here.

00:59:00.400 --> 00:59:01.585
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:59:01.585 --> 00:59:03.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:03.160 --> 00:59:05.140
Yeah, that would make it
very difficult-- yeah,

00:59:05.140 --> 00:59:05.860
you know what?

00:59:05.860 --> 00:59:07.000
I'm looking at it now.

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:08.185
Thank you for bringing that.

00:59:08.185 --> 00:59:08.810
Oh, by the way.

00:59:08.810 --> 00:59:11.680
Also, if you can't hear me
back there, then let me know.

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:13.628
Because I can be loud.

00:59:13.628 --> 00:59:14.170
You're right.

00:59:14.170 --> 00:59:16.750
I don't know-- thank you
for some feedback there.

00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:17.410
Let me just--

00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:19.390
I will now write it legibly.

00:59:32.680 --> 00:59:33.440
There we go.

00:59:33.440 --> 00:59:34.980
Can you see that?

00:59:34.980 --> 00:59:35.730
OK.

00:59:35.730 --> 00:59:37.760
Is it positive semidefinite?

00:59:40.580 --> 00:59:43.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:43.870 --> 00:59:45.730
It's not.

00:59:45.730 --> 00:59:47.200
Yes?

00:59:47.200 --> 00:59:49.960
I seem to recall property
that both the diagonals have

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:51.960
to be greater than
every other [INAUDIBLE]..

00:59:51.960 --> 00:59:52.570
There you go.

00:59:52.570 --> 00:59:53.970
That'll do the trick.

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:58.200
That every point in
a-- every element

00:59:58.200 --> 01:00:00.480
of a positive
semidefinite matrix

01:00:00.480 --> 01:00:03.330
is less than or equal
to the geometric mean

01:00:03.330 --> 01:00:05.520
of the associated
diagonal elements.

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:07.450
Everybody remember that or--

01:00:07.450 --> 01:00:07.950
OK.

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:08.610
Apparently not.

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:09.360
But that's fine.

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:10.980
Everything's cool.

01:00:10.980 --> 01:00:12.300
OK.

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:15.510
And here, that would say,
1 better be less than

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:18.360
or equal to the square
root of 0 times 0.

01:00:18.360 --> 01:00:19.720
That's not true.

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:22.760
So that's not
positive semidefinite.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:26.375
OK, so I think we
said about-- anyway,

01:00:26.375 --> 01:00:27.950
it's an interesting function.

01:00:27.950 --> 01:00:30.500
There's a graph
of it over there.

01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:32.010
OK.

01:00:32.010 --> 01:00:32.905
Log-sum-exp.

01:00:32.905 --> 01:00:34.030
This is an interesting one.

01:00:34.030 --> 01:00:36.450
This comes up in tons of
fields with completely

01:00:36.450 --> 01:00:37.230
different names.

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:39.220
Every field thinks
they invented it.

01:00:39.220 --> 01:00:43.410
So, I think it's called
softmax in some fields.

01:00:43.410 --> 01:00:46.150
OK, somebody give
me some other names.

01:00:46.150 --> 01:00:48.620
I think there's some
weird esoteric ones.

01:00:48.620 --> 01:00:51.020
Want to hear a
weird esoteric one?

01:00:51.020 --> 01:00:52.625
And this is in full dialect.

01:00:52.625 --> 01:00:53.540
Boltzmann function.

01:00:53.540 --> 01:00:54.380
What is it?

01:00:54.380 --> 01:00:55.280
Boltzmann function.

01:00:55.280 --> 01:00:55.822
There you go.

01:00:55.822 --> 01:00:57.550
Boltzmann function.

01:00:57.550 --> 01:00:58.700
I didn't know that.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:00.230
The Boltzmann what?

01:01:00.230 --> 01:01:02.000
Boltzmann [INAUDIBLE] function.

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:02.540
OK, great.

01:01:02.540 --> 01:01:06.740
I mean that's-- yeah,
that's highly pedantic.

01:01:06.740 --> 01:01:12.230
OK, so this function, here's
one from in a strong dialect.

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:15.680
This is the dB
combining formula.

01:01:15.680 --> 01:01:16.790
That's from EE.

01:01:16.790 --> 01:01:19.430
You may have friends
or eventually you

01:01:19.430 --> 01:01:22.190
may at some point in your life
have friends who are in EE.

01:01:22.190 --> 01:01:24.230
So, probably worth
your while to speak

01:01:24.230 --> 01:01:26.930
a little bit of the dialect
or at least recognize

01:01:26.930 --> 01:01:28.550
when people are speaking it.

01:01:28.550 --> 01:01:31.310
So decibel is some weird
thing from the 1920s.

01:01:31.310 --> 01:01:35.450
It's like the 220 times
log base 10 of a--

01:01:35.450 --> 01:01:36.110
anyway.

01:01:36.110 --> 01:01:38.340
So it turns out
that's the formula.

01:01:38.340 --> 01:01:41.450
So if you don't mind, I'm
going to go into full EE.

01:01:41.450 --> 01:01:43.850
And you'd say-- if you walk
up to any electrical engineer

01:01:43.850 --> 01:01:45.392
and you say, well,
I have all these--

01:01:45.392 --> 01:01:47.310
I have these powers coming in.

01:01:47.310 --> 01:01:50.220
I have this one coming
in at minus 11 dBm,

01:01:50.220 --> 01:01:52.200
one is coming in at
plus 13, one at 14.

01:01:52.200 --> 01:01:54.450
And if I add up all the
power, what's the total power?

01:01:54.450 --> 01:01:57.600
And they would know.

01:01:57.600 --> 01:01:59.657
By the way, does anyone
speak that dialect?

01:01:59.657 --> 01:02:00.240
Somebody does.

01:02:00.240 --> 01:02:01.620
OK, we have one.

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:03.960
You speak a little bit?

01:02:03.960 --> 01:02:04.470
OK.

01:02:04.470 --> 01:02:06.000
Did you did you
grow up speaking it

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:09.030
at home or something or what?

01:02:09.030 --> 01:02:10.260
You did?

01:02:10.260 --> 01:02:10.980
OK, OK.

01:02:10.980 --> 01:02:11.610
Fine, OK.

01:02:11.610 --> 01:02:13.290
So, I know other dialects.

01:02:13.290 --> 01:02:14.220
I can try-- I can--

01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:15.570
OK, fine.

01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:16.320
OK.

01:02:16.320 --> 01:02:18.570
So, it's actually a
really cool function.

01:02:18.570 --> 01:02:21.172
OK, number one, it's
convex, which I'm not

01:02:21.172 --> 01:02:22.380
going to go into the details.

01:02:22.380 --> 01:02:25.772
But the reason you
figure that out is--

01:02:25.772 --> 01:02:27.480
actually, it's an
interesting derivation.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:28.440
It goes like this.

01:02:28.440 --> 01:02:29.710
You work out the Hessian.

01:02:29.710 --> 01:02:30.810
It's a sum of two terms.

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:33.990
And you look over here and
you say, well, let's see.

01:02:33.990 --> 01:02:40.330
It's kind of-- so z is this
x-- so these are non-negative.

01:02:40.330 --> 01:02:41.580
You'd say, well, I don't know.

01:02:41.580 --> 01:02:43.163
That's a positive
semidefinite matrix.

01:02:43.163 --> 01:02:45.000
And then what you're
hoping the simple thing

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:47.880
would be the Hessian is
one positive semidefinite

01:02:47.880 --> 01:02:48.847
matrix plus another.

01:02:48.847 --> 01:02:51.180
And then you'd say, the sum
of two positive semidefinite

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:54.060
matrices-- but it's not to
be-- there's a minus sign here.

01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:55.500
And this rank one thing.

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:57.090
It still is positive
semidefinite.

01:02:57.090 --> 01:02:58.830
It's just you have
to show it, which

01:02:58.830 --> 01:03:00.330
I'm not going to
go into the details

01:03:00.330 --> 01:03:02.080
here about how you do that.

01:03:02.080 --> 01:03:04.260
But anyway.

01:03:04.260 --> 01:03:05.520
And it's a very cool function.

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:07.260
It's actually
basically like it's--

01:03:07.260 --> 01:03:09.390
I think I like softmax.

01:03:09.390 --> 01:03:12.275
I don't know who invented
that, but maybe, I don't know.

01:03:12.275 --> 01:03:13.650
Maybe people in
machine learning.

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:14.192
I don't know.

01:03:14.192 --> 01:03:17.580
So that's my
preferred term for it.

01:03:17.580 --> 01:03:20.850
Softmax means it's kind
of a smooth approximation

01:03:20.850 --> 01:03:21.655
of the max.

01:03:21.655 --> 01:03:24.030
I mean, if you've seen this
before-- how many people have

01:03:24.030 --> 01:03:25.750
seen this before somewhere?

01:03:25.750 --> 01:03:26.250
Yeah.

01:03:26.250 --> 01:03:28.170
So, OK.

01:03:28.170 --> 01:03:34.230
So, it's like if one of the
x's is, let's say, a factor--

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:36.120
not a factor, but
let's say it's 1x

01:03:36.120 --> 01:03:41.370
is bigger than all the others
by, let's say, a gap of 3.

01:03:41.370 --> 01:03:43.590
Then when you
exponentiate them, it

01:03:43.590 --> 01:03:47.160
means that that 1 is now
more than E cubed bigger

01:03:47.160 --> 01:03:47.998
than all the others.

01:03:47.998 --> 01:03:49.290
So when you add up the others--

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:51.457
I mean, it depends, I'm
just being very rough here--

01:03:51.457 --> 01:03:54.210
most of it comes
from that one term.

01:03:54.210 --> 01:03:56.297
And then you add them
up, you take the log,

01:03:56.297 --> 01:03:58.380
puts you back on the other
scale and you get that.

01:03:58.380 --> 01:04:02.040
So, OK, here's another
one, is the geometric mean

01:04:02.040 --> 01:04:03.400
in many variables.

01:04:03.400 --> 01:04:07.740
So the n-th root of a
bunch of positive numbers

01:04:07.740 --> 01:04:10.920
or non-negative numbers,
that's a concave function.

01:04:10.920 --> 01:04:12.660
And you'd have a similar thing.

01:04:12.660 --> 01:04:15.820
So, OK.

01:04:15.820 --> 01:04:20.110
So let me briefly mention the
connection between convex sets

01:04:20.110 --> 01:04:24.100
and-- because right now you
have the convexity now applies

01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:26.440
to two things.

01:04:26.440 --> 01:04:29.260
You have convexity
of a set and you

01:04:29.260 --> 01:04:31.360
have convexity of a function.

01:04:31.360 --> 01:04:35.260
And so, obviously, people would
not have used the same term

01:04:35.260 --> 01:04:37.390
if there was not a connection.

01:04:37.390 --> 01:04:40.390
The connection might not be
quite what you think it is.

01:04:40.390 --> 01:04:42.260
Oh, by the way, we
have concave functions.

01:04:42.260 --> 01:04:43.690
We don't have concave sets.

01:04:43.690 --> 01:04:45.500
No such thing.

01:04:45.500 --> 01:04:49.000
I mean, you could say that,
but it would be weird.

01:04:49.000 --> 01:04:52.520
So, I'm not even sure
what it would be,

01:04:52.520 --> 01:04:54.567
but maybe the complement
of a convex set.

01:04:54.567 --> 01:04:56.150
I don't know what
it would-- actually,

01:04:56.150 --> 01:04:58.970
I don't know what it would be.

01:04:58.970 --> 01:05:01.760
So don't say that because
it has no meaning.

01:05:01.760 --> 01:05:02.720
OK.

01:05:02.720 --> 01:05:05.700
So, there's a connection
between these things.

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:09.500
So the very important one is
the concept of a sublevel set.

01:05:09.500 --> 01:05:12.140
Now, a sublevel
set of a function

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:14.898
is the set of all points
where the function

01:05:14.898 --> 01:05:15.940
value is less than alpha.

01:05:15.940 --> 01:05:18.020
That's actually
super interesting.

01:05:18.020 --> 01:05:22.220
When f becomes an objective
in an optimization problem,

01:05:22.220 --> 01:05:25.070
this is basically,
these are all the things

01:05:25.070 --> 01:05:28.490
that perform at the
level alpha and better.

01:05:28.490 --> 01:05:31.040
Because I'm minimizing
f, let's say.

01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:32.960
So that's the sublevel set.

01:05:32.960 --> 01:05:35.270
It makes perfect sense.

01:05:35.270 --> 01:05:40.190
Or it can have the super level
set, something like that.

01:05:40.190 --> 01:05:42.230
OK, now it turns out
that the sublevel sets

01:05:42.230 --> 01:05:45.380
of convex functions are convex.

01:05:45.380 --> 01:05:48.170
So if you have a convex function
and I look at the sublevel set,

01:05:48.170 --> 01:05:50.880
it's going to be convex.

01:05:50.880 --> 01:05:53.040
OK.

01:05:53.040 --> 01:05:54.270
Let's see.

01:05:54.270 --> 01:05:57.840
The real connection is this, is
through the so-called epigraph.

01:05:57.840 --> 01:06:02.490
"Epi" means above, graph
means, I guess, just graph,

01:06:02.490 --> 01:06:04.420
which means a
drawing or whatever.

01:06:04.420 --> 01:06:07.020
And so remember that if
I have a function on Rn,

01:06:07.020 --> 01:06:10.830
its graph is a set in Rn plus 1.

01:06:10.830 --> 01:06:15.750
It's a set of all points
of the form x, t, where--

01:06:15.750 --> 01:06:18.210
well, x is in the domain
and f of x equals t.

01:06:18.210 --> 01:06:20.520
That's actually the graph.

01:06:20.520 --> 01:06:24.210
The epigraph is everything
that lies above the graph.

01:06:24.210 --> 01:06:26.040
And so this is a function f.

01:06:26.040 --> 01:06:30.660
This dark curve is what
we'd call its graph.

01:06:30.660 --> 01:06:32.310
Well, here n is 1.

01:06:32.310 --> 01:06:33.630
So it's in R2.

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:35.460
And the epigraph is
this shaded thing.

01:06:35.460 --> 01:06:36.315
That's the epigraph.

01:06:38.920 --> 01:06:39.930
And it's interesting.

01:06:39.930 --> 01:06:44.220
It's everything that's bigger
than or equal to the function

01:06:44.220 --> 01:06:46.110
value at that point.

01:06:46.110 --> 01:06:47.460
So that's the epigraph.

01:06:47.460 --> 01:06:49.620
And it turns out a
function is convex if

01:06:49.620 --> 01:06:52.690
and only if epi f
is a convex set.

01:06:52.690 --> 01:06:55.110
So that's actually
the connection

01:06:55.110 --> 01:06:57.780
between the concepts
of convexity of a set

01:06:57.780 --> 01:06:59.940
and convexity of a function.

01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:03.450
So, I mean, there's
not that much here.

01:07:03.450 --> 01:07:05.220
But yeah, I mean,
we're already getting

01:07:05.220 --> 01:07:07.660
close to interesting things.

01:07:07.660 --> 01:07:11.160
So, let's fit a
statistical model

01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:12.493
by minimizing the negative.

01:07:12.493 --> 01:07:14.160
If you understand
what I'm saying, fine.

01:07:14.160 --> 01:07:15.452
If you don't, that's cool, too.

01:07:15.452 --> 01:07:16.450
We'll do this later.

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:18.390
Let's fit a statistical model.

01:07:18.390 --> 01:07:21.280
X is going to be the set of
parameters in your model.

01:07:21.280 --> 01:07:24.090
And I want to minimize the
negative log likelihood.

01:07:24.090 --> 01:07:25.200
Everybody following me?

01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:28.230
I mean, it's fine if you don't.

01:07:28.230 --> 01:07:28.770
OK.

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:29.940
And you would get
something and that

01:07:29.940 --> 01:07:32.340
would be like the, whatever,
the maximum likelihood estimate

01:07:32.340 --> 01:07:33.173
of those parameters.

01:07:33.173 --> 01:07:34.050
Everybody cool?

01:07:34.050 --> 01:07:35.010
OK.

01:07:35.010 --> 01:07:39.090
But then I could look
at the sublevel sets

01:07:39.090 --> 01:07:40.650
of the negative log likelihood.

01:07:40.650 --> 01:07:44.755
Or if you like, the super level
sets of the log likelihood.

01:07:44.755 --> 01:07:47.130
So again, if you're trained
in statistics or probability,

01:07:47.130 --> 01:07:48.540
if you know what-- that's fine.

01:07:48.540 --> 01:07:51.810
Those are actually super
interesting things.

01:07:51.810 --> 01:07:56.970
Those are the sets of parameters
for which your log likelihood

01:07:56.970 --> 01:07:59.380
exceeds some minimum value.

01:07:59.380 --> 01:08:01.410
And if that's near
the top, so you

01:08:01.410 --> 01:08:03.420
would get something like this.

01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:07.020
Here's a log likelihood.

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:10.050
This would be the
maximum likelihood point.

01:08:10.050 --> 01:08:13.800
The super level sets
are super interesting.

01:08:13.800 --> 01:08:15.992
It's these points here.

01:08:15.992 --> 01:08:18.450
And they would have a beautiful
statistical interpretation.

01:08:18.450 --> 01:08:24.020
Because if you're close to
the maximum likelihood, then

01:08:24.020 --> 01:08:26.920
it would be very hard for
someone to say your model

01:08:26.920 --> 01:08:27.770
is different.

01:08:27.770 --> 01:08:29.740
In fact, if you
were to actually try

01:08:29.740 --> 01:08:33.550
to figure out which hypothesis
was correct, is it this

01:08:33.550 --> 01:08:35.319
or is it that point?

01:08:35.319 --> 01:08:39.609
You could actually talk about
how close they would be.

01:08:39.609 --> 01:08:40.689
So it's already getting--

01:08:40.689 --> 01:08:42.819
by the way, what are
the-- if you take--

01:08:42.819 --> 01:08:45.250
this is just for fun since I
started on this weird thing.

01:08:45.250 --> 01:08:47.470
But that's the maximum
likelihood point.

01:08:47.470 --> 01:08:49.660
If you'd just back
off a tiny bit

01:08:49.660 --> 01:08:53.319
from the maximum likelihood,
what do those super level sets

01:08:53.319 --> 01:08:54.040
look like?

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:54.970
Just for fun.

01:08:54.970 --> 01:08:57.790
If you're in statistics, and I
know those people in statistics

01:08:57.790 --> 01:08:58.473
here.

01:08:58.473 --> 01:09:00.890
And you should even know this
if you're not in statistics.

01:09:00.890 --> 01:09:03.260
So, what do they look like?

01:09:03.260 --> 01:09:05.740
What are the sets of
parameter values that

01:09:05.740 --> 01:09:09.670
are almost maximum likelihood?

01:09:09.670 --> 01:09:10.170
Yeah.

01:09:10.170 --> 01:09:11.045
Confidence intervals.

01:09:11.045 --> 01:09:11.609
They're what?

01:09:11.609 --> 01:09:12.998
Confidence intervals.

01:09:12.998 --> 01:09:14.790
Yeah, I mean, they're
confidence intervals.

01:09:14.790 --> 01:09:17.802
Yeah, but I'm talking
about in many dimensions.

01:09:17.802 --> 01:09:19.760
And what do they look
like, what's their shape?

01:09:19.760 --> 01:09:21.578
They look like ellipsoids.

01:09:21.578 --> 01:09:23.120
They are indeed
confidence intervals.

01:09:23.120 --> 01:09:24.560
They look like ellipsoids.

01:09:24.560 --> 01:09:27.649
And in fact, the curvature is
given by the Fisher information

01:09:27.649 --> 01:09:28.700
matrix.

01:09:28.700 --> 01:09:31.220
So sorry, that was too weird.

01:09:31.220 --> 01:09:32.510
I shouldn't have done--

01:09:32.510 --> 01:09:35.569
this stuff is so boring and
dry at this point in the class,

01:09:35.569 --> 01:09:36.540
I can't help myself.

01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:37.670
So I apologize.

01:09:37.670 --> 01:09:39.020
That was just a weird thing.

01:09:39.020 --> 01:09:39.529
OK.

01:09:39.529 --> 01:09:40.552
So, fine.

01:09:40.552 --> 01:09:41.760
We'll get back to that later.

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:42.890
Don't worry.

01:09:42.890 --> 01:09:46.850
OK, so Jensen's inequality.

01:09:46.850 --> 01:09:49.819
Jensen was a mathematician
who kind of popularized

01:09:49.819 --> 01:09:52.933
the concept of convexity
about 120 years ago.

01:09:52.933 --> 01:09:54.350
I mean, basically,
actually people

01:09:54.350 --> 01:09:56.570
had had all these sort
of inequalities known,

01:09:56.570 --> 01:09:58.790
and they used them to
prove all sorts of things.

01:09:58.790 --> 01:10:00.445
All he did was codify it.

01:10:00.445 --> 01:10:01.820
And he said, hey,
I just realized

01:10:01.820 --> 01:10:04.710
that these 14 different people
are using the same argument.

01:10:04.710 --> 01:10:06.740
And so I'm going to
introduce a concept called

01:10:06.740 --> 01:10:08.810
convexity and an inequality.

01:10:08.810 --> 01:10:10.340
OK.

01:10:10.340 --> 01:10:12.470
So here's Jensen's inequality.

01:10:12.470 --> 01:10:15.320
Jensen's inequality is just--
it's just the inequality that

01:10:15.320 --> 01:10:16.640
defines a convex function.

01:10:16.640 --> 01:10:19.670
It says, a function
at the mixture

01:10:19.670 --> 01:10:22.170
is less than or equal to the
mixture of the function values.

01:10:22.170 --> 01:10:24.200
This is so-called
Jensen's inequality.

01:10:24.200 --> 01:10:27.800
Well, the extension,
you can extend this

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:30.530
all the way to expectation.

01:10:30.530 --> 01:10:32.060
And it would go like this.

01:10:32.060 --> 01:10:36.680
Suppose z is a random variable,
then its f of expectation

01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:41.540
is less than or equal to
the expectation of f of z,

01:10:41.540 --> 01:10:42.750
for any random variable.

01:10:42.750 --> 01:10:44.333
And by the way, this
is a special case

01:10:44.333 --> 01:10:47.210
of this, where I just
have the probability--

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:50.240
I just have z is
concentrated on two points.

01:10:50.240 --> 01:10:53.330
It's either x or it's y
with probability theta

01:10:53.330 --> 01:10:55.610
and 1 minus theta, respectively.

01:10:55.610 --> 01:10:57.735
So that's Jensen's inequality.

01:10:57.735 --> 01:10:59.610
And actually, it's a
super interesting thing.

01:10:59.610 --> 01:11:01.880
It basically tells
you, let's see.

01:11:01.880 --> 01:11:04.190
OK, so I might think
of it this way.

01:11:04.190 --> 01:11:10.790
You could say that dithering
hurts or actually risk hurts.

01:11:10.790 --> 01:11:13.067
So let me just draw
a picture of that.

01:11:13.067 --> 01:11:14.900
And this will get clearer
later in the class

01:11:14.900 --> 01:11:15.858
when we do other stuff.

01:11:15.858 --> 01:11:17.285
But let's just try this out.

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:23.550
Yeah, so here's a
convex function.

01:11:23.550 --> 01:11:25.920
And then I make a little
probability distribution.

01:11:25.920 --> 01:11:26.820
It looks like that.

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:28.620
There.

01:11:28.620 --> 01:11:29.520
This is x here.

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:31.740
Here's my little
probability distribution.

01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:36.600
And I want to know, what happens
to the-- what happens over here

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:38.670
to the--

01:11:38.670 --> 01:11:42.285
if this is x and this is,
let's say, y equals f of x.

01:11:42.285 --> 01:11:44.160
I get a new distribution on y.

01:11:44.160 --> 01:11:45.990
It looks like, I don't know.

01:11:45.990 --> 01:11:48.300
It looks like that.

01:11:48.300 --> 01:11:51.090
And now I want to-- let's
do some calculations here.

01:11:51.090 --> 01:11:53.340
What it says, let's
suppose that x is actually

01:11:53.340 --> 01:11:55.800
this nominal value
plus something that's

01:11:55.800 --> 01:11:59.220
0 mean that wiggles like that.

01:11:59.220 --> 01:12:04.210
Well, what you do is if
this were affine like that,

01:12:04.210 --> 01:12:06.150
we all know what happens
to a random variable

01:12:06.150 --> 01:12:08.940
when you pass it through
an affine function.

01:12:08.940 --> 01:12:10.710
Variance maps the right way.

01:12:10.710 --> 01:12:12.630
But the super
interesting thing here

01:12:12.630 --> 01:12:15.450
is now what we're doing
is sometimes we're above,

01:12:15.450 --> 01:12:18.090
sometimes we're
below this value.

01:12:18.090 --> 01:12:20.460
But what convexity
says, or at least

01:12:20.460 --> 01:12:22.320
if you understand
what it means locally,

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:25.320
it says that if
you're above or below,

01:12:25.320 --> 01:12:27.390
I mean, there's the
affine estimate of what

01:12:27.390 --> 01:12:29.820
happens to you, but
you're always worse--

01:12:29.820 --> 01:12:33.150
well, again, you're
always larger than that.

01:12:33.150 --> 01:12:35.520
And so what that
makes perfect-- then

01:12:35.520 --> 01:12:39.360
this thing makes perfect sense,
that you have this value,

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:41.190
but you wiggle around sometimes.

01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:44.460
And that says you
have this value here,

01:12:44.460 --> 01:12:47.550
but you also wiggle around that.

01:12:47.550 --> 01:12:50.790
But when you wiggle
around like that,

01:12:50.790 --> 01:12:53.350
actually, it always goes up.

01:12:53.350 --> 01:12:56.155
Because even if you go down
here, you're above this line.

01:12:56.155 --> 01:12:57.780
And if you go up,
you're above it, too.

01:12:57.780 --> 01:13:00.390
OK, so maybe that
didn't make any sense,

01:13:00.390 --> 01:13:02.920
but that's just my
interpretation of this.

01:13:02.920 --> 01:13:04.590
So this is just a
very basic thing.

01:13:04.590 --> 01:13:08.580
It's Jensen's inequality.

01:13:08.580 --> 01:13:09.510
OK.

01:13:09.510 --> 01:13:12.572
So at this point, let me
explain a few things about how

01:13:12.572 --> 01:13:13.780
this is all going to go down.

01:13:13.780 --> 01:13:15.480
And this is actually
the beginning

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:18.722
of how we move toward
something reasonable.

01:13:18.722 --> 01:13:20.430
If this were a math
course, we could just

01:13:20.430 --> 01:13:21.690
go for the rest of
the quarter, and I

01:13:21.690 --> 01:13:23.370
could show you
interesting convex sets

01:13:23.370 --> 01:13:25.260
and go off on weird
tangents and stuff

01:13:25.260 --> 01:13:27.398
like that, which should be fine.

01:13:27.398 --> 01:13:28.440
No, it wouldn't actually.

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:30.000
But anyway.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:32.812
OK, so the way we're
going to do this is we're

01:13:32.812 --> 01:13:34.020
going to do this like a calc.

01:13:34.020 --> 01:13:35.452
It's going to be a calculus.

01:13:35.452 --> 01:13:37.410
Oh, and by the way, it's
not just the right way

01:13:37.410 --> 01:13:39.510
to think of it
mathematically, it's

01:13:39.510 --> 01:13:41.580
going to turn out this
is also what makes

01:13:41.580 --> 01:13:43.800
this a 100% actionable in code.

01:13:43.800 --> 01:13:47.040
So, everything we're
about to say is math,

01:13:47.040 --> 01:13:49.020
but it's going to
be-- it's actually

01:13:49.020 --> 01:13:51.558
going to be backed up in code.

01:13:51.558 --> 01:13:53.100
And it's actually
going to be the key

01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:55.890
to a whole lot of stuff.

01:13:55.890 --> 01:13:58.080
OK, so let's go
over these things.

01:13:58.080 --> 01:14:00.030
How do you find out if
a function is convex?

01:14:00.030 --> 01:14:04.147
Well, every now and
then, extremely rarely,

01:14:04.147 --> 01:14:06.480
and you should do this only
if you're absolutely pushed,

01:14:06.480 --> 01:14:07.860
if you have no
other option, it's

01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:12.070
basically OK, let x be in the
domain, let y be in the domain,

01:14:12.070 --> 01:14:16.420
let theta be between 0 and 1, f
of theta x plus 1 minus theta y

01:14:16.420 --> 01:14:18.920
and then a lot of math and
argument and stuff like that.

01:14:18.920 --> 01:14:23.030
So don't do that
if you can get it--

01:14:23.030 --> 01:14:25.720
if you can avoid that.

01:14:25.720 --> 01:14:29.950
If it's twice differentiable,
you can compute the Hessian.

01:14:29.950 --> 01:14:30.790
You know what?

01:14:30.790 --> 01:14:32.140
Don't do that either.

01:14:32.140 --> 01:14:35.680
I mean, unless it's
something super simple.

01:14:35.680 --> 01:14:38.810
But generally speaking, that's
not the right way to do it.

01:14:38.810 --> 01:14:42.370
I mean, it is for showing
log determinant or--

01:14:42.370 --> 01:14:44.120
no, sorry, that's a bad example.

01:14:44.120 --> 01:14:46.540
So, log determinant is
a really good example.

01:14:46.540 --> 01:14:49.930
So if someone says, show log
determinant as concave, you go,

01:14:49.930 --> 01:14:51.490
not a problem.

01:14:51.490 --> 01:14:54.550
All I have to do is
calculate the Hessian

01:14:54.550 --> 01:14:56.740
of the log determinant
with respect to a matrix.

01:14:56.740 --> 01:14:58.150
Everybody following this?

01:14:58.150 --> 01:15:01.540
Well, OK, matrix already
has two entries--

01:15:01.540 --> 01:15:02.260
two indices.

01:15:02.260 --> 01:15:03.820
It's indexed by i and j.

01:15:03.820 --> 01:15:05.500
The Hessian is indexed by 4.

01:15:05.500 --> 01:15:08.170
That's i, j, k, l.

01:15:08.170 --> 01:15:11.020
Now, in my experience, the
only people who were actually

01:15:11.020 --> 01:15:13.420
able to actually
work with tensors,

01:15:13.420 --> 01:15:16.600
like things with four
indices, are people

01:15:16.600 --> 01:15:18.490
in mechanical engineering.

01:15:18.490 --> 01:15:19.870
So they can do it.

01:15:19.870 --> 01:15:21.820
These are like weird
stress strain tensors.

01:15:21.820 --> 01:15:25.450
Some physicists can do it, or at
least they say they can do it.

01:15:25.450 --> 01:15:29.020
But honestly, normal
people can't do that.

01:15:29.020 --> 01:15:31.780
And how on earth would you
write down some crazy--

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:33.580
I said, well, show me
my 100 by 100 matrix

01:15:33.580 --> 01:15:34.750
is positive semidefinite.

01:15:34.750 --> 01:15:36.940
Let's work out the Hessian.

01:15:36.940 --> 01:15:39.970
There's 5,000 entries, so
it's a 5,000 by 5-- anyway,

01:15:39.970 --> 01:15:41.860
it just gets very unattractive.

01:15:41.860 --> 01:15:43.270
Everybody following this?

01:15:43.270 --> 01:15:43.960
OK.

01:15:43.960 --> 01:15:45.190
All right.

01:15:45.190 --> 01:15:47.860
OK, this is the
right way to do it.

01:15:47.860 --> 01:15:48.970
And it's a calculus.

01:15:48.970 --> 01:15:50.890
And the way you
do that is we will

01:15:50.890 --> 01:15:52.960
have a bunch of atoms,
things that we know

01:15:52.960 --> 01:15:54.080
are convex functions.

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:54.580
Here's one.

01:15:54.580 --> 01:15:57.400
Log is concave, exp is convex.

01:15:57.400 --> 01:16:00.778
Log-sum-exp is convex, log
determinant is concave.

01:16:00.778 --> 01:16:02.320
So you're going to
have these things.

01:16:02.320 --> 01:16:04.630
You're going to have--
there's not that many of them.

01:16:04.630 --> 01:16:06.250
20, 30, 50, I don't know.

01:16:06.250 --> 01:16:07.090
It's not that many.

01:16:07.090 --> 01:16:08.680
You will know all of them.

01:16:08.680 --> 01:16:11.460
I mean, you'll know
a bunch of them.

01:16:11.460 --> 01:16:14.070
And then you're going to
learn calculus rules, which is

01:16:14.070 --> 01:16:16.260
how you combine these things.

01:16:16.260 --> 01:16:20.250
And so some of these are going
to be mind numbingly simple.

01:16:20.250 --> 01:16:21.900
Like here's one.

01:16:21.900 --> 01:16:24.600
The sum of two convex
functions is convex.

01:16:24.600 --> 01:16:26.743
Well, I mean, first
of all, it's obvious.

01:16:26.743 --> 01:16:29.160
Draw a picture in your head,
draw a function that goes up,

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:30.785
another one goes up,
add them together.

01:16:30.785 --> 01:16:31.680
It still curves up.

01:16:31.680 --> 01:16:33.180
Like, hello, of course.

01:16:33.180 --> 01:16:34.860
So, anyway, that's one.

01:16:34.860 --> 01:16:37.003
But some of these
are going to be--

01:16:37.003 --> 01:16:39.420
we're going to see some very
interesting ones that are not

01:16:39.420 --> 01:16:41.910
obvious in some of these rules.

01:16:41.910 --> 01:16:44.520
Not only that, it's going
to turn out that at least

01:16:44.520 --> 01:16:48.120
all these four, which we
teach as separate rules,

01:16:48.120 --> 01:16:50.560
it turns out, weirdly,
they're all the same rule,

01:16:50.560 --> 01:16:51.840
which we'll get to.

01:16:51.840 --> 01:16:57.420
Not only that, it's the main
rule behind constructing code

01:16:57.420 --> 01:17:00.445
where you can verify a
function is convex or concave,

01:17:00.445 --> 01:17:02.070
which we will get to
later and you will

01:17:02.070 --> 01:17:03.840
be doing in about two weeks.

01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:06.420
So this is the program.

01:17:06.420 --> 01:17:10.660
It's constructive
convexity analysis.

01:17:10.660 --> 01:17:12.640
That's what this is.

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:18.250
So, no gradients are harmed in
the proving of convexity, which

01:17:18.250 --> 01:17:21.730
you don't want to do anyway.

01:17:21.730 --> 01:17:24.850
And no one is going to--

01:17:24.850 --> 01:17:27.250
you're not going to go back
to the basic definition.

01:17:27.250 --> 01:17:29.270
So, we'll look at
a couple of these

01:17:29.270 --> 01:17:30.520
and then we'll quit for today.

01:17:30.520 --> 01:17:34.000
But this is the
point at which it'll

01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:37.090
start transitioning to something
that you can actually--

01:17:37.090 --> 01:17:38.470
that's actionable.

01:17:38.470 --> 01:17:39.040
OK.

01:17:39.040 --> 01:17:42.654
So we'll start simple.

01:17:42.654 --> 01:17:44.830
Here.

01:17:44.830 --> 01:17:46.600
Here's one.

01:17:46.600 --> 01:17:49.870
3.7 times a convex
function, that's convex.

01:17:49.870 --> 01:17:52.300
Well, OK, whatever.

01:17:52.300 --> 01:17:53.722
The curvature just
went up by 3.7.

01:17:53.722 --> 01:17:55.930
If it was non-negative
before, and now it's even more

01:17:55.930 --> 01:17:58.630
non-negative, if that makes
any sense, which it doesn't.

01:17:58.630 --> 01:18:01.150
But OK, that's fine.

01:18:01.150 --> 01:18:01.720
The sum.

01:18:01.720 --> 01:18:03.490
Sum of two convex--
and by the way,

01:18:03.490 --> 01:18:05.776
somebody has to prove these.

01:18:05.776 --> 01:18:07.493
These are one liners.

01:18:07.493 --> 01:18:09.410
I'll tell you a couple
that aren't one liners,

01:18:09.410 --> 01:18:11.300
but these are one liners.

01:18:11.300 --> 01:18:12.170
Here's one.

01:18:12.170 --> 01:18:14.460
Precomposition with
an affine function.

01:18:14.460 --> 01:18:18.050
So, if I have an affine
function, that's ax plus b,

01:18:18.050 --> 01:18:21.830
and I take that and then I
pump it into a convex function,

01:18:21.830 --> 01:18:25.010
this is a function of
x and it's also convex.

01:18:25.010 --> 01:18:27.140
And that's not hard to show.

01:18:27.140 --> 01:18:29.420
I mean, it kind of makes sense.

01:18:29.420 --> 01:18:30.920
OK.

01:18:30.920 --> 01:18:32.420
Let's look at some examples.

01:18:32.420 --> 01:18:35.840
So this function is going to
come up later in the class.

01:18:35.840 --> 01:18:37.370
It's called the log barrier.

01:18:37.370 --> 01:18:41.270
Its domain is all x's for
which all of these numbers

01:18:41.270 --> 01:18:41.960
are positive.

01:18:41.960 --> 01:18:44.240
I mean, otherwise, the
log doesn't mean anything.

01:18:44.240 --> 01:18:48.590
Oh, in this class, the
domain of log is R plus plus.

01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:51.050
So, if you're taking a
class on complex analysis,

01:18:51.050 --> 01:18:52.340
that's not true.

01:18:52.340 --> 01:18:54.540
So it just depends on
the social context.

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:57.800
But here, when I write
log determinant, log,

01:18:57.800 --> 01:18:58.730
that's what it means.

01:19:02.300 --> 01:19:05.330
OK, so here, this is the
so-called log barrier

01:19:05.330 --> 01:19:09.270
of a set of linear inequalities.

01:19:09.270 --> 01:19:12.990
And all the inequalities
have to be strict.

01:19:12.990 --> 01:19:16.710
In other words, you better have
ai transpose x less than b.

01:19:16.710 --> 01:19:20.460
Otherwise, this log
doesn't even make sense.

01:19:20.460 --> 01:19:22.020
And so that's the log barrier.

01:19:22.020 --> 01:19:24.300
And it turns out that's
going to be convex.

01:19:24.300 --> 01:19:25.710
It's got a minus in front.

01:19:25.710 --> 01:19:26.610
Why?

01:19:26.610 --> 01:19:28.560
And the rule would go
something like this.

01:19:28.560 --> 01:19:29.460
Well, let's see.

01:19:29.460 --> 01:19:34.110
This is an affine function
with a concave function.

01:19:34.110 --> 01:19:36.120
And then concave of affine.

01:19:36.120 --> 01:19:38.730
I'm saying it the
way you will say it.

01:19:38.730 --> 01:19:40.920
And you will think it by week 3.

01:19:40.920 --> 01:19:42.270
This is the way you will think.

01:19:42.270 --> 01:19:46.650
You'll say, concave
of affine is concave.

01:19:46.650 --> 01:19:51.690
Then sum of concave is concave.

01:19:51.690 --> 01:19:54.090
Minus converts
concave to convex.

01:19:54.090 --> 01:19:55.290
Everybody following this?

01:19:55.290 --> 01:19:55.790
OK.

01:19:55.790 --> 01:19:57.945
So you will be doing this
without even thinking.

01:19:57.945 --> 01:19:59.820
It's going to be like
parsing English syntax.

01:19:59.820 --> 01:20:01.112
This is how it's going to work.

01:20:01.112 --> 01:20:03.390
And we're going to talk
at very high levels,

01:20:03.390 --> 01:20:07.110
and you're going to say things
like that and not even--

01:20:07.110 --> 01:20:09.660
that's the way
it's going to work.

01:20:09.660 --> 01:20:10.330
OK.

01:20:10.330 --> 01:20:13.690
So, I think what we'll
do is we'll quit here.

01:20:13.690 --> 01:20:16.227
We'll finish this off next time.

01:20:16.227 --> 01:20:18.560
And by the end of next lecture,
by the end of this week,

01:20:18.560 --> 01:20:20.980
you will have survived, maybe.

01:20:20.980 --> 01:20:22.860
We'll see.

